		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		h2 id="introduction" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1"
			}
			- " Introduction"
			a href="#introduction" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		h3 id="abstract" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.1"
			}
			- " Where does this specification fit?"
			a href="#abstract" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This specification defines a big part of the web platform, in lots of detail. Its place in the\n  web platform specification stack relative to other specifications can be best summed up as\n  follows:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		svg id="abstractimg" width="398" role="img" viewBox="0 0 398 359" height="359" aria-label="It consists of everything else, above such core technologies as HTTP, TLS, DOM, Unicode, Web IDL, MIME, URL, XML, JavaScript, and Encoding; below higher-level technologies like CSS, SVG, MathML, and Service Workers; and to the side of technologies like IndexedDB, Fetch, CSP, AV1, Opus, and PNG." {
			- "\n   "
			rect width="398" height="80"
			- "\n   "
			text class="horizontal" x="199" y="45" {
				- "CSS SVG MathML Service Workers"
			}
			- "\n   "
			rect width="67" y="85" height="177"
			- "\n   "
			text transform="translate(25 173.5) rotate(-90)" class="left" {
				- "IDB Fetch CSP"
			}
			- "\n   "
			text transform="translate(50 173.5) rotate(-90)" class="left" {
				- "AV1 Opus PNG"
			}
			- "\n   \n   "
			image xlink:href="/images/abstract.jpeg" width="326" x="72" y="85" height="177"
			- "\n   "
			text class="right" x="130" y="250" {
				- "THIS SPECIFICATION"
			}
			- "\n   "
			rect width="398" y="267" height="92"
			- "\n   "
			text class="horizontal" x="199" y="300" {
				- "HTTP TLS DOM Unicode Web IDL"
			}
			- "\n   "
			text class="horizontal" x="199" y="330" {
				- "MIME URL XML JavaScript Encoding"
			}
			- "\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n\n  "
		h3 id="is-this-html5?" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.2"
			}
			- " Is this HTML5?"
			a href="#is-this-html5?" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In short: Yes."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In more length: the term \"HTML5\" is widely used as a buzzword to refer to modern web\n  technologies, many of which (though by no means all) are developed at the WHATWG. This document is\n  one such; others are available from "
			a href="https://spec.whatwg.org/" {
				- "the WHATWG Standards\n  overview"
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n\n  "
		h3 id="background" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.3"
			}
			- " Background"
			a href="#background" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "HTML is the World Wide Web's core markup language. Originally, HTML was primarily designed as a\n  language for semantically describing scientific documents. Its general design, however, has\n  enabled it to be adapted, over the subsequent years, to describe a number of other types of\n  documents and even applications."
		}
		- "\n\n\n  "
		h3 id="audience" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.4"
			}
			- " Audience"
			a href="#audience" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This specification is intended for authors of documents and scripts that use the features\n  defined in this specification, implementers of tools that operate on pages that\n  use the features defined in this specification, and individuals wishing to establish the\n  correctness of documents or implementations with respect to the requirements of this\n  specification."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This document is probably not suited to readers who do not already have at least a passing\n  familiarity with web technologies, as in places it sacrifices clarity for precision, and brevity\n  for completeness. More approachable tutorials and authoring guides can provide a gentler\n  introduction to the topic."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In particular, familiarity with the basics of DOM is necessary for a complete understanding of\n  some of the more technical parts of this specification. An understanding of Web IDL, HTTP, XML,\n  Unicode, character encodings, JavaScript, and CSS will also be helpful in places but is not\n  essential."
		}
		- "\n\n\n  "
		h3 id="scope" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.5"
			}
			- " Scope"
			a href="#scope" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This specification is limited to providing a semantic-level markup language and associated\n  semantic-level scripting APIs for authoring accessible pages on the web ranging from static\n  documents to dynamic applications."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The scope of this specification does not include providing mechanisms for media-specific\n  customization of presentation (although default rendering rules for web browsers are included at\n  the end of this specification, and several mechanisms for hooking into CSS are provided as part of\n  the language)."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The scope of this specification is not to describe an entire operating system. In particular,\n  hardware configuration software, image manipulation tools, and applications that users would be\n  expected to use with high-end workstations on a daily basis are out of scope. In terms of\n  applications, this specification is targeted specifically at applications that would be expected\n  to be used by users on an occasional basis, or regularly but from disparate locations, with low\n  CPU requirements. Examples of such applications include online purchasing systems, searching\n  systems, games (especially multiplayer online games), public telephone books or address books,\n  communications software (email clients, instant messaging clients, discussion software), document\n  editing software, etc."
		}
		- "\n\n\n  "
		h3 id="history-2" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.6"
			}
			- " History"
			a href="#history-2" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "For its first five years (1990-1995), HTML went through a number of revisions and experienced a\n  number of extensions, primarily hosted first at CERN, and then at the IETF."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "With the creation of the W3C, HTML's development changed venue again. A first abortive attempt\n  at extending HTML in 1995 known as HTML 3.0 then made way to a more pragmatic approach known as\n  HTML 3.2, which was completed in 1997. HTML4 quickly followed later that same year."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The following year, the W3C membership decided to stop evolving HTML and instead begin work on\n  an XML-based equivalent, called XHTML.  This\n  effort started with a reformulation of HTML4 in XML, known as XHTML 1.0, which added no new\n  features except the new serialization, and which was completed in 2000. After XHTML 1.0, the W3C's\n  focus turned to making it easier for other working groups to extend XHTML, under the banner of\n  XHTML Modularization. In parallel with this, the W3C also worked on a new language that was not\n  compatible with the earlier HTML and XHTML languages, calling it XHTML2."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Around the time that HTML's evolution was stopped in 1998, parts of the API for HTML developed\n  by browser vendors were specified and published under the name DOM Level 1 (in 1998) and DOM Level\n  2 Core and DOM Level 2 HTML (starting in 2000 and culminating in 2003). These efforts then petered\n  out, with some DOM Level 3 specifications published in 2004 but the working group being closed\n  before all the Level 3 drafts were completed."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In 2003, the publication of XForms, a technology which was positioned as the next generation of\n  web forms, sparked a renewed interest in evolving HTML itself, rather than finding replacements\n  for it. This interest was borne from the realization that XML's deployment as a web technology was\n  limited to entirely new technologies (like RSS and later Atom), rather than as a replacement for\n  existing deployed technologies (like HTML)."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "A proof of concept to show that it was possible to extend HTML4's forms to provide many of the\n  features that XForms 1.0 introduced, without requiring browsers to implement rendering engines\n  that were incompatible with existing HTML web pages, was the first result of this renewed\n  interest. At this early stage, while the draft was already publicly available, and input was\n  already being solicited from all sources, the specification was only under Opera Software's\n  copyright."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The idea that HTML's evolution should be reopened was tested at a W3C workshop in 2004, where\n  some of the principles that underlie the HTML5 work (described below), as well as the\n  aforementioned early draft proposal covering just forms-related features, were presented to the\n  W3C jointly by Mozilla and Opera. The proposal was rejected on the grounds that the proposal\n  conflicted with the previously chosen direction for the web's evolution; the W3C staff and\n  membership voted to continue developing XML-based replacements instead."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Shortly thereafter, Apple, Mozilla, and Opera jointly announced their intent to continue\n  working on the effort under the umbrella of a new venue called the WHATWG. A public mailing list\n  was created, and the draft was moved to the WHATWG site. The copyright was subsequently amended to\n  be jointly owned by all three vendors, and to allow reuse of the specification."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The WHATWG was based on several core principles, in particular that technologies need to be\n  backwards compatible, that specifications and implementations need to match even if this means\n  changing the specification rather than the implementations, and that specifications need to be\n  detailed enough that implementations can achieve complete interoperability without\n  reverse-engineering each other."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The latter requirement in particular required that the scope of the HTML5 specification include\n  what had previously been specified in three separate documents: HTML4, XHTML1, and DOM2 HTML. It\n  also meant including significantly more detail than had previously been considered the norm."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In 2006, the W3C indicated an interest to participate in the development of HTML5 after all,\n  and in 2007 formed a working group chartered to work with the WHATWG on the development of the\n  HTML5 specification. Apple, Mozilla, and Opera allowed the W3C to publish the specification under\n  the W3C copyright, while keeping a version with the less restrictive license on the WHATWG\n  site."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "For a number of years, both groups then worked together. In 2011, however, the groups came to\n  the conclusion that they had different goals: the W3C wanted to publish a \"finished\" version of\n  \"HTML5\", while the WHATWG wanted to continue working on a Living Standard for HTML, continuously\n  maintaining the specification rather than freezing it in a state with known problems, and adding\n  new features as needed to evolve the platform."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In 2019, the WHATWG and W3C "
			a href="https://www.w3.org/blog/news/archives/7753" {
				- "signed an\n  agreement"
			}
			- " to collaborate on a single version of HTML going forward: this document."
		}
		- "\n\n\n\n  "
		h3 id="design-notes" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.7"
			}
			- " Design notes"
			a href="#design-notes" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "It must be admitted that many aspects of HTML appear at first glance to be nonsensical and\n  inconsistent."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "HTML, its supporting DOM APIs, as well as many of its supporting technologies, have been\n  developed over a period of several decades by a wide array of people with different priorities\n  who, in many cases, did not know of each other's existence."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Features have thus arisen from many sources, and have not always been designed in especially\n  consistent ways. Furthermore, because of the unique characteristics of the web, implementation\n  bugs have often become de-facto, and now de-jure, standards, as content is often unintentionally\n  written in ways that rely on them before they can be fixed."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Despite all this, efforts have been made to adhere to certain design goals. These are described\n  in the next few subsections."
		}
		- "\n\n\n  \n\n  "
		h4 id="serialisability-of-script-execution" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.7.1"
			}
			- " Serializability of script execution"
			a href="#serialisability-of-script-execution" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "To avoid exposing web authors to the complexities of multithreading, the HTML and DOM APIs are\n  designed such that no script can ever detect the simultaneous execution of other scripts. Even\n  with "
			a href="#worker" id="serialisability-of-script-execution:worker" {
				- "workers"
			}
			- ", the intent is that the behavior of implementations can\n  be thought of as completely serializing the execution of all scripts in all globals."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The exception to this general design principle is the JavaScript "
			code id="serialisability-of-script-execution:sharedarraybuffer" {
				a data-x-internal="sharedarraybuffer" href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-sharedarraybuffer-objects" {
					- "SharedArrayBuffer"
				}
			}
			- "\n  class. Using "
			code id="serialisability-of-script-execution:sharedarraybuffer-2" {
				a data-x-internal="sharedarraybuffer" href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-sharedarraybuffer-objects" {
					- "SharedArrayBuffer"
				}
			}
			- " objects, it can in fact be observed that scripts in\n  other "
			a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-agents" id="serialisability-of-script-execution:agent" data-x-internal="agent" {
				- "agents"
			}
			- " are executing simultaneously. Furthermore, due to the\n  JavaScript memory model, there are situations which not only are un-representable via serialized\n  "
			em {
				- "script"
			}
			- " execution, but also un-representable via serialized "
			em {
				- "statement"
			}
			- " execution\n  among those scripts."
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n\n\n  "
		h4 id="compliance-with-other-specifications" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.7.2"
			}
			- " Compliance with other specifications"
			a href="#compliance-with-other-specifications" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This specification interacts with and relies on a wide variety of other specifications. In\n  certain circumstances, unfortunately, conflicting needs have led to this specification violating\n  the requirements of these other specifications. Whenever this has occurred, the transgressions\n  have each been noted as a \""
			dfn id="willful-violation" {
				- "willful violation"
			}
			- "\", and the reason for the violation has\n  been noted."
		}
		- "\n\n\n\n  "
		h4 id="extensibility" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.7.3"
			}
			- " Extensibility"
			a href="#extensibility" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "HTML has a wide array of extensibility mechanisms that can be used for adding semantics in a\n  safe manner:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors can use the "
					code id="extensibility:classes" {
						a href="#classes" {
							- "class"
						}
					}
					- " attribute to extend elements,\n   effectively creating their own elements, while using the most applicable existing \"real\" HTML\n   element, so that browsers and other tools that don't know of the extension can still support it\n   somewhat well. This is the tack used by microformats, for example."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors can include data for inline client-side scripts or server-side site-wide scripts\n   to process using the "
					code id="extensibility:attr-data-*" {
						a href="#attr-data-*" {
							- "data-*=\"\""
						}
					}
					- " attributes. These are guaranteed\n   to never be touched by browsers, and allow scripts to include data on HTML elements that scripts\n   can then look for and process."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors can use the "
					code id="extensibility:the-meta-element" {
						a href="#the-meta-element" {
							- "<meta name=\"\" content=\"\">"
						}
					}
					- " mechanism to\n   include page-wide metadata."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors can use the "
					code id="extensibility:attr-hyperlink-rel" {
						a href="#attr-hyperlink-rel" {
							- "rel=\"\""
						}
					}
					- " mechanism to annotate\n   links with specific meanings by registering "
					a href="#concept-rel-extensions" id="extensibility:concept-rel-extensions" {
						- "extensions to\n   the predefined set of link types"
					}
					- ". This is also used by microformats."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors can embed raw data using the "
					code id="extensibility:the-script-element" {
						a href="#the-script-element" {
							- "<script type=\"\">"
						}
					}
					- "\n   mechanism with a custom type, for further handling by inline or server-side scripts."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors can extend APIs using the JavaScript prototyping mechanism. This is widely used by\n   script libraries, for instance."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors can use the microdata feature (the "
					code id="extensibility:attr-itemscope" {
						a href="#attr-itemscope" {
							- "itemscope=\"\""
						}
					}
					- " and "
					code id="extensibility:names:-the-itemprop-attribute" {
						a href="#names:-the-itemprop-attribute" {
							- "itemprop=\"\""
						}
					}
					- "\n   attributes) to embed nested name-value pairs of data to be shared with other applications and\n   sites."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors can define, share, and use "
					a href="#custom-element" id="extensibility:custom-element" {
						- "custom elements"
					}
					- "\n   to extend the vocabulary of HTML. The requirements of "
					a href="#valid-custom-element-name" id="extensibility:valid-custom-element-name" {
						- "valid custom element names"
					}
					- " ensure forward compatibility (since no elements will be\n   added to HTML, SVG, or MathML with hyphen-containing local names in the future)."
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n\n\n\n  "
		h3 id="html-vs-xhtml" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.8"
			}
			- " HTML vs XML syntax"
			a href="#html-vs-xhtml" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This specification defines an abstract language for describing documents and applications, and\n  some APIs for interacting with in-memory representations of resources that use this language."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The in-memory representation is known as \"DOM HTML\", or \"the DOM\" for short."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "There are various concrete syntaxes that can be used to transmit resources that use this\n  abstract language, two of which are defined in this specification."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The first such concrete syntax is the HTML syntax. This is the format suggested for most\n  authors. It is compatible with most legacy web browsers. If a document is transmitted with the\n  "
			code id="html-vs-xhtml:text/html" {
				a href="#text/html" {
					- "text/html"
				}
			}
			- " "
			a id="html-vs-xhtml:mime-type" href="https://mimesniff.spec.whatwg.org/#mime-type" data-x-internal="mime-type" {
				- "MIME type"
			}
			- ", then it will be processed as an HTML document by\n  web browsers. This specification defines the latest HTML syntax, known simply as \"HTML\"."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The second concrete syntax is XML. When a document is transmitted with an "
			a id="html-vs-xhtml:xml-mime-type" href="https://mimesniff.spec.whatwg.org/#xml-mime-type" data-x-internal="xml-mime-type" {
				- "XML MIME\n  type"
			}
			- ", such as "
			code id="html-vs-xhtml:application/xhtml+xml" {
				a href="#application/xhtml+xml" {
					- "application/xhtml+xml"
				}
			}
			- ", then it is treated as an XML document by\n  web browsers, to be parsed by an XML processor. Authors are reminded that the processing for XML\n  and HTML differs; in particular, even minor syntax errors will prevent a document labeled as XML\n  from being rendered fully, whereas they would be ignored in the HTML syntax."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="note" {
			- "The XML syntax for HTML was formerly referred to as \"XHTML\", but this\n  specification does not use that term (among other reasons, because no such term is used for the\n  HTML syntaxes of MathML and SVG)."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The DOM, the HTML syntax, and the XML syntax cannot all represent the same content. For\n  example, namespaces cannot be represented using the HTML syntax, but they are supported in the DOM\n  and in the XML syntax. Similarly, documents that use the "
			code id="html-vs-xhtml:the-noscript-element" {
				a href="#the-noscript-element" {
					- "noscript"
				}
			}
			- " feature can be\n  represented using the HTML syntax, but cannot be represented with the DOM or in the XML syntax.\n  Comments that contain the string \""
			code {
				- "-->"
			}
			- "\" can only be represented in the\n  DOM, not in the HTML and XML syntaxes."
		}
		- "\n\n\n  "
		h3 id="structure-of-this-specification" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.9"
			}
			- " Structure of this specification"
			a href="#structure-of-this-specification" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This specification is divided into the following major sections:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		dl {
			dt {
				a href="#introduction" {
					- "Introduction"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "Non-normative materials providing a context for the HTML standard."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#infrastructure" {
					- "Common infrastructure"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "The conformance classes, algorithms, definitions, and the common underpinnings of the rest of\n   the specification."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#dom" {
					- "Semantics, structure, and APIs of HTML documents"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "Documents are built from elements. These elements form a tree using the DOM. This section\n   defines the features of this DOM, as well as introducing the features common to all elements, and\n   the concepts used in defining elements."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#semantics" {
					- "The elements of HTML"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "Each element has a predefined meaning, which is explained in this section. Rules for authors\n   on how to use the element, along with user agent requirements for how to\n   handle each element, are also given. This includes large signature features of HTML such\n   as video playback and subtitles, form controls and form submission, and a 2D graphics API known\n   as the HTML canvas."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#microdata" {
					- "Microdata"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "This specification introduces a mechanism for adding machine-readable annotations to\n   documents, so that tools can extract trees of name-value pairs from the document. This section\n   describes this mechanism and some algorithms that can be used to convert HTML\n   documents into other formats. This section also defines some sample Microdata vocabularies\n   for contact information, calendar events, and licensing works."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#editing" {
					- "User interaction"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "HTML documents can provide a number of mechanisms for users to interact with and modify\n   content, which are described in this section, such as how focus works, and drag-and-drop."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#browsers" {
					- "Loading web pages"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "HTML documents do not exist in a vacuum — this section defines many of the features\n   that affect environments that deal with multiple pages, such as web browsers."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#webappapis" {
					- "Web application APIs"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "This section introduces basic features for scripting of applications in HTML."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#workers" {
					- "Web workers"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "This section defines an API for background threads in JavaScript."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#worklets" {
					- "Worklets"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "This section defines infrastructure for APIs that need to run JavaScript separately from the\n   main JavaScript execution environment."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#comms" {
					- "The communication APIs"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "This section describes some mechanisms that applications written in HTML can use to\n   communicate with other applications from different domains running on the same client. It also\n   introduces a server-push event stream mechanism known as Server Sent Events or\n   "
				code id="structure-of-this-specification:eventsource" {
					a href="#eventsource" {
						- "EventSource"
					}
				}
				- ", and a two-way full-duplex socket protocol for scripts known as Web\n   Sockets."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#webstorage" {
					- "Web storage"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "This section defines a client-side storage mechanism based on name-value pairs."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#syntax" {
					- "The HTML syntax"
				}
			}
			dt {
				a href="#xhtml" {
					- "The XML syntax"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "All of these features would be for naught if they couldn't be represented in a serialized\n   form and sent to other people, and so these sections define the syntaxes of HTML and XML, along with rules for how to parse content using those syntaxes."
			}
			dt {
				a href="#rendering" {
					- "Rendering"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "This section defines the default rendering rules for web browsers."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "There are also some appendices, listing "
			a href="#obsolete" {
				- "obsolete features"
			}
			- " and "
			a href="#iana" {
				- "IANA considerations"
			}
			- ", and several indices."
		}
		- "\n\n\n  \n\n  "
		h4 id="how-to-read-this-specification" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.9.1"
			}
			- " How to read this specification"
			a href="#how-to-read-this-specification" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This specification should be read like all other specifications. First, it should be read\n  cover-to-cover, multiple times. Then, it should be read backwards at least once. Then it should be\n  read by picking random sections from the contents list and following all the cross-references."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "As described in the conformance requirements section below, this specification describes\n  conformance criteria for a variety of conformance classes. In particular, there are conformance\n  requirements that apply to "
			em {
				- "producers"
			}
			- ", for example authors and the documents they create,\n  and there are conformance requirements that apply to "
			em {
				- "consumers"
			}
			- ", for example web browsers.\n  They can be distinguished by what they are requiring: a requirement on a producer states what is\n  allowed, while a requirement on a consumer states how software is to act."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		div class="example" {
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "For example, \"the "
				code {
					- "foo"
				}
				- " attribute's value must be a "
				a href="#valid-integer" id="how-to-read-this-specification:valid-integer" {
					- "valid\n   integer"
				}
				- "\" is a requirement on producers, as it lays out the allowed values; in contrast,\n   the requirement \"the "
				code {
					- "foo"
				}
				- " attribute's value must be parsed using the\n   "
				a href="#rules-for-parsing-integers" id="how-to-read-this-specification:rules-for-parsing-integers" {
					- "rules for parsing integers"
				}
				- "\" is a requirement on consumers, as it describes how to\n   process the content."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			strong {
				- "Requirements on producers have no bearing whatsoever on consumers."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		div class="example" {
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "Continuing the above example, a requirement stating that a particular attribute's value is\n   constrained to being a "
				a href="#valid-integer" id="how-to-read-this-specification:valid-integer-2" {
					- "valid integer"
				}
				- " emphatically does "
				em {
					- "not"
				}
				- " imply anything\n   about the requirements on consumers. It might be that the consumers are in fact required to treat\n   the attribute as an opaque string, completely unaffected by whether the value conforms to the\n   requirements or not. It might be (as in the previous example) that the consumers are required to\n   parse the value using specific rules that define how invalid (non-numeric in this case) values\n   are to be processed."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n\n\n  "
		h4 id="typographic-conventions" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.9.2"
			}
			- " Typographic conventions"
			a href="#typographic-conventions" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This is a definition, requirement, or explanation."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="note" {
			- "This is a note."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="example" {
			- "This is an example."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="XXX" {
			- "This is an open issue."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="warning" {
			- "This is a warning."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre class="extract" {
			code class="idl" {
				- "["
				c- g="" {
					- "Exposed"
				}
				- "="
				c- n="" {
					- "Window"
				}
				- "]\n"
				c- b="" {
					- "interface"
				}
				- " "
				dfn {
					c- g="" {
						- "Example"
					}
				}
				- " {\n  // this is an IDL definition\n};"
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		dl class="domintro" {
			dt {
				code {
					var {
						- "variable"
					}
					- " = "
					var {
						- "object"
					}
					- "."
					a href="#x-that" id="typographic-conventions:x-that" {
						- "method"
					}
					- "(["
					var {
						- "optionalArgument"
					}
					- "])"
				}
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "This is a note to authors describing the usage of an interface."
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="css" {
				c- c="" {
					- "/* this is a CSS fragment */"
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The defining instance of a term is marked up like "
			dfn id="x-this" {
				- "this"
			}
			- ". Uses of that\n  term are marked up like "
			a href="#x-this" id="typographic-conventions:x-this" {
				- "this"
			}
			- " or like "
			i id="typographic-conventions:x-this-2" {
				a href="#x-this" {
					- "this"
				}
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The defining instance of an element, attribute, or API is marked up like "
			dfn id="x-that" {
				code {
					- "this"
				}
			}
			- ". References to that element, attribute, or API are marked up\n  like "
			code id="typographic-conventions:x-that-2" {
				a href="#x-that" {
					- "this"
				}
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Other code fragments are marked up "
			code {
				- "like this"
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Variables are marked up like "
			var {
				- "this"
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In an algorithm, steps in "
			a href="#synchronous-section" id="typographic-conventions:synchronous-section" {
				- "synchronous\n  sections"
			}
			- " are marked with ⌛."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In some cases, requirements are given in the form of lists with conditions and corresponding\n  requirements. In such cases, the requirements that apply to a condition are always the first set\n  of requirements that follow the condition, even in the case of there being multiple sets of\n  conditions for those requirements. Such cases are presented as follows:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		dl class="switch" {
			dt {
				- "This is a condition\n   "
			}
			dt {
				- "This is another condition\n   "
			}
			dd {
				- "This is the requirement that applies to the conditions above.\n\n   "
			}
			dt {
				- "This is a third condition\n   "
			}
			dd {
				- "This is the requirement that applies to the third condition.\n  "
			}
		}
		- "\n\n\n\n\n  "
		h3 id="a-quick-introduction-to-html" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "1.10"
			}
			- " A quick introduction to HTML"
			a href="#a-quick-introduction-to-html" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "A basic HTML document looks like this:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre id="intro-early-example" {
			code class="html" {
				c- cp="" {
					- "<!DOCTYPE html>"
				}
				- "\n"
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "html"
				}
				- " "
				c- e="" {
					- "lang"
				}
				c- o="" {
					- "="
				}
				c- s="" {
					- "\"en\""
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "\n "
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "head"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "\n  "
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "title"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "Sample page"
				c- p="" {
					- "</"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "title"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "\n "
				c- p="" {
					- "</"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "head"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "\n "
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "body"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "\n  "
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "h1"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "Sample page"
				c- p="" {
					- "</"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "h1"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "\n  "
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "p"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "This is a "
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "a"
				}
				- " "
				c- e="" {
					- "href"
				}
				c- o="" {
					- "="
				}
				c- s="" {
					- "\"demo.html\""
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "simple"
				c- p="" {
					- "</"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "a"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- " sample."
				c- p="" {
					- "</"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "p"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "\n  "
				c- c="" {
					- "<!-- this is a comment -->"
				}
				- "\n "
				c- p="" {
					- "</"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "body"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "\n"
				c- p="" {
					- "</"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "html"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "HTML documents consist of a tree of elements and text. Each element is denoted in the source by\n  a "
			a href="#syntax-start-tag" id="a-quick-introduction-to-html:syntax-start-tag" {
				- "start tag"
			}
			- ", such as \""
			code {
				- "<body>"
			}
			- "\", and\n  an "
			a href="#syntax-end-tag" id="a-quick-introduction-to-html:syntax-end-tag" {
				- "end tag"
			}
			- ", such as \""
			code {
				- "</body>"
			}
			- "\".\n  (Certain start tags and end tags can in certain cases be "
			a href="#syntax-tag-omission" id="a-quick-introduction-to-html:syntax-tag-omission" {
				- "omitted"
			}
			- " and are implied by other tags.)"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Tags have to be nested such that elements are all completely within each other, without\n  overlapping:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre class="bad" {
			code class="html" {
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "p"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "This is "
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
