					}
					c- p="" {
						- ">"
					}
				}
			}
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "Here, the "
				code {
					- "data-has-payment-request"
				}
				- " attribute is effectively being used\n   as a "
				a href="#boolean-attribute" id="embedding-custom-non-visible-data-with-the-data-*-attributes:boolean-attribute" {
					- "boolean attribute"
				}
				- "; it is enough to check the presence of the attribute.\n   However, if the author so wishes, it could later be populated with some value, maybe to indicate\n   limited functionality of the feature."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Every "
			a href="#html-elements" id="embedding-custom-non-visible-data-with-the-data-*-attributes:html-elements-2" {
				- "HTML element"
			}
			- " may have any number of "
			a href="#custom-data-attribute" id="embedding-custom-non-visible-data-with-the-data-*-attributes:custom-data-attribute-3" {
				- "custom data attributes"
			}
			- " specified, with any value."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Authors should carefully design such extensions so that when the attributes are ignored and any\n  associated CSS dropped, the page is still usable."
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		p {
			- "User agents must not derive any implementation behavior from these attributes or values.\n  Specifications intended for user agents must not define these attributes to have any meaningful\n  values."
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		p {
			- "JavaScript libraries may use the "
			a href="#custom-data-attribute" id="embedding-custom-non-visible-data-with-the-data-*-attributes:custom-data-attribute-4" {
				- "custom data\n  attributes"
			}
			- ", as they are considered to be part of the page on which they are used. Authors\n  of libraries that are reused by many authors are encouraged to include their name in the attribute\n  names, to reduce the risk of clashes. Where it makes sense, library authors are also encouraged to\n  make the exact name used in the attribute names customizable, so that libraries whose authors\n  unknowingly picked the same name can be used on the same page, and so that multiple versions of a\n  particular library can be used on the same page even when those versions are not mutually\n  compatible."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		div class="example" {
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "For example, a library called \"DoQuery\" could use attribute names like "
				code {
					- "data-doquery-range"
				}
				- ", and a library called \"jJo\" could use attributes names like\n   "
				code {
					- "data-jjo-range"
				}
				- ". The jJo library could also provide an API to set which\n   prefix to use (e.g. "
				code {
					- "J.setDataPrefix('j2')"
				}
				- ", making the attributes have names\n   like "
				code {
					- "data-j2-range"
				}
				- ")."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
		}
