		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The "
			code id="the-section-element:the-section-element" {
				a href="#the-section-element" {
					- "section"
				}
			}
			- " element "
			a href="#represents" id="the-section-element:represents" {
				- "represents"
			}
			- " a generic section of a document or\n  application. A section, in this context, is a thematic grouping of content, typically with a\n  heading."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="example" {
			- "Examples of sections would be chapters, the various tabbed pages in a tabbed\n  dialog box, or the numbered sections of a thesis. A web site's home page could be split into\n  sections for an introduction, news items, and contact information."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="note" {
			- "Authors are encouraged to use the "
			code id="the-section-element:the-article-element" {
				a href="#the-article-element" {
					- "article"
				}
			}
			- " element instead of the\n  "
			code id="the-section-element:the-section-element-2" {
				a href="#the-section-element" {
					- "section"
				}
			}
			- " element when it would make sense to syndicate the contents of the\n  element."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p id="use-div-for-wrappers" class="note" {
			- "The "
			code id="the-section-element:the-section-element-3" {
				a href="#the-section-element" {
					- "section"
				}
			}
			- " element is not a generic\n  container element. When an element is needed only for styling purposes or as a convenience for\n  scripting, authors are encouraged to use the "
			code id="the-section-element:the-div-element" {
				a href="#the-div-element" {
					- "div"
				}
			}
			- " element instead. A general rule is\n  that the "
			code id="the-section-element:the-section-element-4" {
				a href="#the-section-element" {
					- "section"
				}
			}
			- " element is appropriate only if the element's contents would be\n  listed explicitly in the document's "
			a href="#outline" id="the-section-element:outline" {
				- "outline"
			}
			- "."
		}
