		- "\n\n\n  "
		h5 id="article-or-section" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.3.12.1"
			}
			- " Article or section?"
			a href="#article-or-section" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "A "
			code id="article-or-section:the-section-element" {
				a href="#the-section-element" {
					- "section"
				}
			}
			- " forms part of something else. An "
			code id="article-or-section:the-article-element" {
				a href="#the-article-element" {
					- "article"
				}
			}
			- " is its own thing.\n  But how does one know which is which? Mostly the real answer is \"it depends on author intent\"."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "For example, one could imagine a book with a \"Granny Smith\" chapter that just said \"These\n  juicy, green apples make a great filling for apple pies.\"; that would be a "
			code id="article-or-section:the-section-element-2" {
				a href="#the-section-element" {
					- "section"
				}
			}
			- "\n  because there'd be lots of other chapters on (maybe) other kinds of apples."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "On the other hand, one could imagine a tweet or reddit comment or tumblr post or newspaper\n  classified ad that just said \"Granny Smith. These juicy, green apples make a great filling for\n  apple pies.\"; it would then be "
			code id="article-or-section:the-article-element-2" {
				a href="#the-article-element" {
					- "article"
				}
			}
			- "s because that was the whole thing."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "A comment on an article is not part of the "
			code id="article-or-section:the-article-element-3" {
				a href="#the-article-element" {
					- "article"
				}
			}
			- " on which it is commenting,\n  therefore it is its own "
			code id="article-or-section:the-article-element-4" {
				a href="#the-article-element" {
					- "article"
				}
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n\n\n  "
