		- "\n\n\n  "
		h6 id="adaptive-images" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.8.4.1.1"
			}
			- " Adaptive images"
			a href="#adaptive-images" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "CSS and media queries can be used to construct graphical page layouts that adapt dynamically to\n  the user's environment, in particular to different "
			a id="adaptive-images:viewport" href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" data-x-internal="viewport" {
				- "viewport"
			}
			- " dimensions and pixel\n  densities. For content, however, CSS does not help; instead, we have the "
			code id="adaptive-images:the-img-element" {
				a href="#the-img-element" {
					- "img"
				}
			}
			- " element's\n  "
			code id="adaptive-images:attr-img-srcset" {
				a href="#attr-img-srcset" {
					- "srcset"
				}
			}
			- " attribute and the "
			code id="adaptive-images:the-picture-element" {
				a href="#the-picture-element" {
					- "picture"
				}
			}
			- " element.\n  This section walks through a sample case showing how to use these features."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Consider a situation where on wide screens (wider than 600 "
			a href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#px" id="adaptive-images:'px'" data-x-internal="'px'" {
				- "CSS\n  pixels"
			}
			- ") a 300×150 image named "
			code {
				- "a-rectangle.png"
			}
			- " is to be used,\n  but on smaller screens (600 "
			a href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#px" id="adaptive-images:'px'-2" data-x-internal="'px'" {
				- "CSS pixels"
			}
			- " and less), a smaller\n  100×100 image called "
			code {
				- "a-square.png"
			}
			- " is to be used. The markup for this\n  would look like this:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="html" {
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
