		- "\n\n\n  \n\n  "
		h5 id="security-and-privacy-considerations" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.8.11.17"
			}
			- " Security and privacy considerations"
			a href="#security-and-privacy-considerations" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The main security and privacy implications of the "
			code id="security-and-privacy-considerations:the-video-element" {
				a href="#the-video-element" {
					- "video"
				}
			}
			- " and "
			code id="security-and-privacy-considerations:the-audio-element" {
				a href="#the-audio-element" {
					- "audio"
				}
			}
			- "\n  elements come from the ability to embed media cross-origin. There are two directions that threats\n  can flow: from hostile content to a victim page, and from a hostile page to victim content."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		hr
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "If a victim page embeds hostile content, the threat is that the content might contain scripted\n  code that attempts to interact with the "
			code id="security-and-privacy-considerations:document" {
				a href="#document" {
					- "Document"
				}
			}
			- " that embeds the content. To avoid\n  this, user agents must ensure that there is no access from the content to the embedding page. In\n  the case of media content that uses DOM concepts, the embedded content must be treated as if it\n  was in its own unrelated "
			a href="#top-level-traversable" id="security-and-privacy-considerations:top-level-traversable" {
				- "top-level traversable"
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="example" {
			- "For instance, if an SVG animation was embedded in a "
			code id="security-and-privacy-considerations:the-video-element-2" {
				a href="#the-video-element" {
					- "video"
				}
			}
			- " element,\n  the user agent would not give it access to the DOM of the outer page. From the perspective of\n  scripts in the SVG resource, the SVG file would appear to be in a lone top-level traversable\n  with no parent."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		hr
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "If a hostile page embeds victim content, the threat is that the embedding page could obtain\n  information from the content that it would not otherwise have access to. The API does expose some\n  information: the existence of the media, its type, its duration, its size, and the performance\n  characteristics of its host. Such information is already potentially problematic, but in practice\n  the same information can more or less be obtained using the "
			code id="security-and-privacy-considerations:the-img-element" {
				a href="#the-img-element" {
					- "img"
				}
			}
			- " element, and so it\n  has been deemed acceptable."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "However, significantly more sensitive information could be obtained if the user agent further\n  exposes metadata within the content, such as subtitles. That information is therefore only exposed\n  if the video resource uses CORS. The "
			code id="security-and-privacy-considerations:attr-media-crossorigin" {
				a href="#attr-media-crossorigin" {
					- "crossorigin"
				}
			}
			- "\n  attribute allows authors to enable CORS. "
			a href="#refsFETCH" {
				- "[FETCH]"
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="example" {
			- "Without this restriction, an attacker could trick a user running within a\n  corporate network into visiting a site that attempts to load a video from a previously leaked\n  location on the corporation's intranet. If such a video included confidential plans for a new\n  product, then being able to read the subtitles would present a serious confidentiality breach."
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n\n  "
		h5 id="best-practices-for-authors-using-media-elements" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.8.11.18"
			}
			- " Best practices for authors using media elements"
			a href="#best-practices-for-authors-using-media-elements" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Playing audio and video resources on small devices such as set-top boxes or mobile phones is\n  often constrained by limited hardware resources in the device. For example, a device might only\n  support three simultaneous videos. For this reason, it is a good practice to release resources\n  held by "
			a href="#media-element" id="best-practices-for-authors-using-media-elements:media-element" {
				- "media elements"
			}
			- " when they are done playing, either by\n  being very careful about removing all references to the element and allowing it to be garbage\n  collected, or, even better, by setting the element's "
			code id="best-practices-for-authors-using-media-elements:attr-media-src" {
				a href="#attr-media-src" {
					- "src"
				}
			}
			- "\n  attribute to an empty string. In cases where "
			code id="best-practices-for-authors-using-media-elements:dom-media-srcobject" {
				a href="#dom-media-srcobject" {
					- "srcObject"
				}
			}
			- "\n  was set, instead set the "
			code id="best-practices-for-authors-using-media-elements:dom-media-srcobject-2" {
				a href="#dom-media-srcobject" {
					- "srcObject"
				}
			}
			- " to null."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Similarly, when the playback rate is not exactly 1.0, hardware, software, or format limitations\n  can cause video frames to be dropped and audio to be choppy or muted."
		}
		- "\n\n\n  \n\n  "
		h5 id="best-practices-for-implementers-of-media-elements" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.8.11.19"
			}
			- " Best practices for implementers of media elements"
			a href="#best-practices-for-implementers-of-media-elements" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "How accurately various aspects of the "
			a href="#media-element" id="best-practices-for-implementers-of-media-elements:media-element" {
				- "media element"
			}
			- " API are implemented is\n  considered a quality-of-implementation issue."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "For example, when implementing the "
			code id="best-practices-for-implementers-of-media-elements:dom-media-buffered" {
				a href="#dom-media-buffered" {
					- "buffered"
				}
			}
			- " attribute,\n  how precise an implementation reports the ranges that have been buffered depends on how carefully\n  the user agent inspects the data. Since the API reports ranges as times, but the data is obtained\n  in byte streams, a user agent receiving a variable-bitrate stream might only be able to determine\n  precise times by actually decoding all of the data. User agents aren't required to do this,\n  however; they can instead return estimates (e.g. based on the average bitrate seen so far) which\n  get revised as more information becomes available."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "As a general rule, user agents are urged to be conservative rather than optimistic. For\n  example, it would be bad to report that everything had been buffered when it had not."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Another quality-of-implementation issue would be playing a video backwards when the codec is\n  designed only for forward playback (e.g. there aren't many key frames, and they are far apart, and\n  the intervening frames only have deltas from the previous frame). User agents could do a poor job,\n  e.g. only showing key frames; however, better implementations would do more work and thus do a\n  better job, e.g. actually decoding parts of the video forwards, storing the complete frames, and\n  then playing the frames backwards."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Similarly, while implementations are allowed to drop buffered data at any time (there is no\n  requirement that a user agent keep all the media data obtained for the lifetime of the media\n  element), it is again a quality of implementation issue: user agents with sufficient resources to\n  keep all the data around are encouraged to do so, as this allows for a better user experience. For\n  example, if the user is watching a live stream, a user agent could allow the user only to view the\n  live video; however, a better user agent would buffer everything and allow the user to seek\n  through the earlier material, pause it, play it forwards and backwards, etc."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		hr
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "When a "
			a href="#media-element" id="best-practices-for-implementers-of-media-elements:media-element-2" {
				- "media element"
			}
			- " that is paused is "
			a href="#remove-an-element-from-a-document" id="best-practices-for-implementers-of-media-elements:remove-an-element-from-a-document" {
				- "removed from a document"
			}
			- " and not reinserted before the next time the "
			a href="#event-loop" id="best-practices-for-implementers-of-media-elements:event-loop" {
				- "event\n  loop"
			}
			- " reaches "
			a href="#step1" {
				- "step 1"
			}
			- ", implementations that are resource constrained are encouraged to take\n  that opportunity to release all hardware resources (like video planes, networking resources, and\n  data buffers) used by the "
			a href="#media-element" id="best-practices-for-implementers-of-media-elements:media-element-3" {
				- "media element"
			}
			- ". (User agents still have to keep track of the\n  playback position and so forth, though, in case playback is later restarted.)"
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n\n\n  "
		h4 id="the-map-element" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.8.12"
			}
			- " The "
			dfn data-dfn-type="element" {
				code {
					- "map"
				}
			}
			- " element"
			a href="#the-map-element" class="self-link"
		}
		div class="mdn-anno wrapped" {
			button onclick="toggleStatus(this)" class="mdn-anno-btn" {
