		- "\n\n\n  "
		h5 id="table-layout-techniques" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.9.1.2"
			}
			- " Techniques for table design"
			a href="#table-layout-techniques" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Good table design is key to making tables more readable and usable."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In visual media, providing column and row borders and alternating row backgrounds can be very\n  effective to make complicated tables more readable."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "For tables with large volumes of numeric content, using monospaced fonts can help users see\n  patterns, especially in situations where a user agent does not render the borders. (Unfortunately,\n  for historical reasons, not rendering borders on tables is a common default.)"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In speech media, table cells can be distinguished by reporting the corresponding headers before\n  reading the cell's contents, and by allowing users to navigate the table in a grid fashion, rather\n  than serializing the entire contents of the table in source order."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Authors are encouraged to use CSS to achieve these effects."
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		p {
			- "User agents are encouraged to render tables using these techniques whenever the page does not\n  use CSS and the table is not classified as a layout table."
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n  "
