		- "\n\n\n  "
		h5 id="implementing-the-server-side-processing-for-a-form" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.10.1.2"
			}
			- " Implementing the server-side processing for a form"
			a href="#implementing-the-server-side-processing-for-a-form" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The exact details for writing a server-side processor are out of scope for this specification.\n  For the purposes of this introduction, we will assume that the script at "
			code {
				- "https://pizza.example.com/order.cgi"
			}
			- " is configured to accept submissions using the\n  "
			code id="implementing-the-server-side-processing-for-a-form:attr-fs-enctype-urlencoded" {
				a href="#attr-fs-enctype-urlencoded" {
					- "application/x-www-form-urlencoded"
				}
			}
			- " format,\n  expecting the following parameters sent in an HTTP POST body:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		dl {
			dt {
				code {
					- "custname"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "Customer's name"
			}
			dt {
				code {
					- "custtel"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "Customer's telephone number"
			}
			dt {
				code {
					- "custemail"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "Customer's email address"
			}
			dt {
				code {
					- "size"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "The pizza size, either "
				code {
					- "small"
				}
				- ", "
				code {
					- "medium"
				}
				- ", or "
				code {
					- "large"
				}
			}
			dt {
				code {
					- "topping"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "A topping, specified once for each selected topping, with the allowed values being "
				code {
					- "bacon"
				}
				- ", "
				code {
					- "cheese"
				}
				- ", "
				code {
					- "onion"
				}
				- ", and "
				code {
					- "mushroom"
				}
			}
			dt {
				code {
					- "delivery"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "The requested delivery time"
			}
			dt {
				code {
					- "comments"
				}
			}
			dd {
				- "The delivery instructions"
			}
		}
		- "\n\n\n  "
		h5 id="configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.10.1.3"
			}
			- " Configuring a form to communicate with a server"
			a href="#configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Form submissions are exposed to servers in a variety of ways, most commonly as HTTP GET or POST\n  requests. To specify the exact method used, the "
			code id="configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server:attr-fs-method" {
				a href="#attr-fs-method" {
					- "method"
				}
			}
			- "\n  attribute is specified on the "
			code id="configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server:the-form-element" {
				a href="#the-form-element" {
					- "form"
				}
			}
			- " element. This doesn't specify how the form data is\n  encoded, though; to specify that, you use the "
			code id="configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server:attr-fs-enctype" {
				a href="#attr-fs-enctype" {
					- "enctype"
				}
			}
			- "\n  attribute. You also have to specify the "
			a id="configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server:url" href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" data-x-internal="url" {
				- "URL"
			}
			- " of the service that will handle the\n  submitted data, using the "
			code id="configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server:attr-fs-action" {
				a href="#attr-fs-action" {
					- "action"
				}
			}
			- " attribute."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "For each form control you want submitted, you then have to give a name that will be used to\n  refer to the data in the submission. We already specified the name for the group of radio buttons;\n  the same attribute ("
			code id="configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server:attr-fe-name" {
				a href="#attr-fe-name" {
					- "name"
				}
			}
			- ") also specifies the submission name.\n  Radio buttons can be distinguished from each other in the submission by giving them different\n  values, using the "
			code id="configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server:attr-input-value" {
				a href="#attr-input-value" {
					- "value"
				}
			}
			- " attribute."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Multiple controls can have the same name; for example, here we give all the checkboxes the same\n  name, and the server distinguishes which checkbox was checked by seeing which values are submitted\n  with that name — like the radio buttons, they are also given unique values with the "
			code id="configuring-a-form-to-communicate-with-a-server:attr-input-value-2" {
				a href="#attr-input-value" {
					- "value"
				}
			}
			- " attribute."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Given the settings in the previous section, this all becomes:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="html" {
				c- p="" {
