		}
		- "\n\n\n\n  "
		h5 id="input-author-notes" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.10.1.8"
			}
			- " Date, time, and number formats"
			a href="#input-author-notes" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n  \n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In this pizza delivery example, the times are specified in the format \"HH:MM\": two digits for\n  the hour, in 24-hour format, and two digits for the time. (Seconds could also be specified, though\n  they are not necessary in this example.)"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In some locales, however, times are often expressed differently when presented to users. For\n  example, in the United States, it is still common to use the 12-hour clock with an am/pm\n  indicator, as in \"2pm\". In France, it is common to separate the hours from the minutes using an\n  \"h\" character, as in \"14h00\"."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Similar issues exist with dates, with the added complication that even the order of the\n  components is not always consistent — for example, in Cyprus the first of February 2003\n  would typically be written \"1/2/03\", while that same date in Japan would typically be written as\n  \"2003年02月01日\" — and even with numbers, where locales differ, for\n  example, in what punctuation is used as the decimal separator and the thousands separator."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "It is therefore important to distinguish the time, date, and number formats used in HTML and in\n  form submissions, which are always the formats defined in this specification (and based on the\n  well-established ISO 8601 standard for computer-readable date and time formats), from the time,\n  date, and number formats presented to the user by the browser and accepted as input from the user\n  by the browser."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The format used \"on the wire\", i.e., in HTML markup and in form submissions, is intended to be\n  computer-readable and consistent irrespective of the user's locale. Dates, for instance, are\n  always written in the format \"YYYY-MM-DD\", as in \"2003-02-01\". While some users might see this\n  format, others might see it as \"01.02.2003\" or \"February 1, 2003\"."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The time, date, or number given by the page in the wire format is then translated to the user's\n  preferred presentation (based on user preferences or on the locale of the page itself), before\n  being displayed to the user. Similarly, after the user inputs a time, date, or number using their\n  preferred format, the user agent converts it back to the wire format before putting it in the DOM\n  or submitting it."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This allows scripts in pages and on servers to process times, dates, and numbers in a\n  consistent manner without needing to support dozens of different formats, while still supporting\n  the users' needs."
		}
		- "\n\n  \n  "
		p class="note" {
			- "See also the "
			a href="#input-impl-notes" {
				- "implementation notes"
			}
			- " regarding\n  localization of form controls."
		}
		- "\n  \n\n\n  "
		h4 id="categories" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.10.2"
			}
			- " Categories"
			a href="#categories" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Mostly for historical reasons, elements in this section fall into several overlapping (but\n  subtly different) categories in addition to the usual ones like "
			a href="#flow-content-2" id="categories:flow-content-2" {
				- "flow content"
			}
			- ",\n  "
			a href="#phrasing-content-2" id="categories:phrasing-content-2" {
				- "phrasing content"
			}
			- ", and "
			a href="#interactive-content-2" id="categories:interactive-content-2" {
				- "interactive content"
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "A number of the elements are "
			dfn id="form-associated-element" {
				- "form-associated\n  elements"
			}
			- ", which means they can have a "
			a href="#form-owner" id="categories:form-owner" {
				- "form owner"
			}
			- ".\n\n  \n  "
		}
		ul class="brief category-list" {
