					}
					- " "
					c- e="" {
						- "accept"
					}
					c- o="" {
						- "="
					}
					c- s="" {
						- "\".doc,.docx,.xml,application/msword,application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document\""
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ">"
					}
				}
			}
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "On platforms that only use file extensions to describe file types, the extensions listed here\n   can be used to filter the allowed documents, while the MIME types can be used with the system's\n   type registration table (mapping MIME types to extensions used by the system), if any, to\n   determine any other extensions to allow. Similarly, on a system that does not have filenames or\n   extensions but labels documents with MIME types internally, the MIME types can be used to pick\n   the allowed files, while the extensions can be used if the system has an extension registration\n   table that maps known extensions to MIME types used by the system."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p class="warning" {
			- "Extensions tend to be ambiguous (e.g. there are an untold number of formats\n  that use the \""
			code {
				- ".dat"
			}
			- "\" extension, and users can typically quite easily rename\n  their files to have a \""
			code {
				- ".doc"
			}
			- "\" extension even if they are not Microsoft Word\n  documents), and MIME types tend to be unreliable (e.g. many formats have no formally registered\n  types, and many formats are in practice labeled using a number of different MIME types). Authors\n  are reminded that, as usual, data received from a client should be treated with caution, as it may\n  not be in an expected format even if the user is not hostile and the user agent fully obeyed the\n  "
			code id="file-upload-state-(type=file):attr-input-accept" {
				a href="#attr-input-accept" {
					- "accept"
				}
			}
			- " attribute's requirements."
		}
