		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		div id="note-dialog-method-names" class="note" {
			- "\n   "
			p {
				- "We use show/close as the verbs for "
				code id="the-dialog-element:the-dialog-element-22" {
					a href="#the-dialog-element" {
						- "dialog"
					}
				}
				- " elements, as opposed to verb pairs that\n   are more commonly thought of as antonyms such as show/hide or open/close, due to the following\n   constraints:"
			}
			- "\n\n   "
			ul {
				li {
					p {
						- "Hiding a dialog is different from closing one. Closing a dialog gives it a return value,\n    fires an event, unblocks the page for other dialogs, and so on. Whereas hiding a dialog is a\n    purely visual property, and is something you can already do with the "
						code id="the-dialog-element:attr-hidden-2" {
							a href="#attr-hidden" {
								- "hidden"
							}
						}
						- " attribute or by removing the "
						code id="the-dialog-element:attr-dialog-open-17" {
							a href="#attr-dialog-open" {
								- "open"
							}
						}
						- " attribute. (See also the "
						a href="#note-dialog-remove-open-attribute" {
							- "note above"
						}
						- " about removing the "
						code id="the-dialog-element:attr-dialog-open-18" {
							a href="#attr-dialog-open" {
								- "open"
							}
						}
						- " attribute, and how hiding the dialog in that way is\n    generally not desired.)"
					}
				}
				li {
					p {
						- "Showing a dialog is different from opening one. Opening a dialog consists of creating\n    and showing that dialog (similar to how "
						code id="the-dialog-element:dom-open" {
							a href="#dom-open" {
								- "window.open()"
							}
						}
						- " both\n    creates and shows a new window). Whereas showing the dialog is the process of taking a\n    "
						code id="the-dialog-element:the-dialog-element-23" {
							a href="#the-dialog-element" {
								- "dialog"
							}
						}
						- " element that is already in the DOM, and making it interactive and visible\n    to the user."
					}
				}
				li {
					p {
						- "If we were to have a "
						code {
							- "dialog.open()"
						}
						- " method despite the above, it\n    would conflict with the "
						code id="the-dialog-element:dom-dialog-open-2" {
							a href="#dom-dialog-open" {
								- "dialog.open"
							}
						}
						- " property."
					}
				}
			}
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "Furthermore, a "
				a href="https://lists.whatwg.org/pipermail/whatwg-whatwg.org/2013-December/041799.html" {
					- "survey"
				}
				- "\n   of many other UI frameworks contemporary to the original design of the "
				code id="the-dialog-element:the-dialog-element-24" {
					a href="#the-dialog-element" {
						- "dialog"
					}
				}
				- "\n   element made it clear that the show/close verb pair was reasonably common."
			}
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "In summary, it turns out that the implications of certain verbs, and how they are used in\n   technology contexts, mean that paired actions such as showing and closing a dialog are not\n   always expressible as antonyms."
			}
			- "\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		hr
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The "
			code id="the-dialog-element:document-2" {
