			}
			- ", which are defined slightly differently and used very\n  differently compared to "
			a href="#autonomous-custom-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:autonomous-custom-element" {
				- "autonomous custom\n  elements"
			}
			- ". They exist to allow reuse of behaviors from the existing elements of HTML, by\n  extending those elements with new custom functionality. This is important since many of the\n  existing behaviors of HTML elements can unfortunately not be duplicated by using purely "
			a href="#autonomous-custom-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:autonomous-custom-element-2" {
				- "autonomous custom elements"
			}
			- ". Instead, "
			a href="#customized-built-in-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:customized-built-in-element-2" {
				- "customized built-in elements"
			}
			- " allow the installation of\n  custom construction behavior, lifecycle hooks, and prototype chain onto existing elements,\n  essentially \"mixing in\" these capabilities on top of the already-existing element."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			a href="#customized-built-in-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:customized-built-in-element-3" {
				- "Customized built-in elements"
			}
			- " require a\n  distinct syntax from "
			a href="#autonomous-custom-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:autonomous-custom-element-3" {
				- "autonomous custom elements"
			}
			- "\n  because user agents and other software key off an element's local name in order to identify the\n  element's semantics and behavior. That is, the concept of "
			a href="#customized-built-in-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:customized-built-in-element-4" {
				- "customized built-in elements"
			}
			- " building on top of existing behavior depends\n  crucially on the extended elements retaining their original local name."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In this example, we'll be creating a "
			a href="#customized-built-in-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:customized-built-in-element-5" {
				- "customized built-in element"
			}
			- " named "
			code {
				- "plastic-button"
			}
			- ", which behaves like a normal button but gets fancy animation\n  effects added whenever you click on it. We start by defining a class, just like before, although\n  this time we extend "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:htmlbuttonelement" {
				a href="#htmlbuttonelement" {
					- "HTMLButtonElement"
				}
			}
			- " instead of "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:htmlelement" {
				a href="#htmlelement" {
					- "HTMLElement"
				}
			}
			- ":"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="js" {
				c- a="" {
					- "class"
				}
				- " PlasticButton "
				c- k="" {
					- "extends"
				}
				- " HTMLButtonElement "
				c- p="" {
					- "{"
				}
				- "\n  constructor"
				c- p="" {
					- "()"
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "{"
				}
				- "\n    "
				c- k="" {
					- "super"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- "();"
				}
				- "\n\n    "
				c- k="" {
					- "this"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "addEventListener"
				c- p="" {
					- "("
				}
				c- u="" {
					- "\"click\""
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ","
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "()"
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "=>"
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "{"
				}
				- "\n      "
				c- c1="" {
					- "// Draw some fancy animation effects!"
				}
				- "\n    "
				c- p="" {
					- "});"
				}
				- "\n  "
				c- p="" {
					- "}"
				}
				- "\n"
				c- p="" {
					- "}"
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "When defining our custom element, we have to also specify the "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:dom-elementdefinitionoptions-extends" {
				a href="#dom-elementdefinitionoptions-extends" {
					- "extends"
				}
			}
			- " option:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="js" {
				- "customElements"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "define"
				c- p="" {
					- "("
				}
				c- u="" {
					- "\"plastic-button\""
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ","
				}
				- " PlasticButton"
				c- p="" {
					- ","
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "{"
				}
				- " "
				c- k="" {
					- "extends"
				}
				c- o="" {
					- ":"
				}
				- " "
				c- u="" {
					- "\"button\""
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "});"
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In general, the name of the element being extended cannot be determined simply by looking at\n  what element interface it extends, as many elements share the same interface (such as\n  "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:the-q-element" {
				a href="#the-q-element" {
					- "q"
				}
			}
			- " and "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:the-blockquote-element" {
				a href="#the-blockquote-element" {
					- "blockquote"
				}
			}
			- " both sharing "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:htmlquoteelement" {
				a href="#htmlquoteelement" {
					- "HTMLQuoteElement"
				}
			}
			- ")."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "To construct our "
			a href="#customized-built-in-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:customized-built-in-element-6" {
				- "customized built-in element"
			}
			- " from parsed HTML source text, we use\n  the "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:attr-is" {
				a href="#attr-is" {
					- "is"
				}
			}
			- " attribute on a "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:the-button-element" {
				a href="#the-button-element" {
					- "button"
				}
			}
			- " element:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="html" {
				c- p="" {
					- "<"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "button"
				}
				- " "
				c- e="" {
					- "is"
				}
				c- o="" {
					- "="
				}
				c- s="" {
					- "\"plastic-button\""
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
				- "Click Me!"
				c- p="" {
					- "</"
				}
				c- f="" {
					- "button"
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ">"
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Trying to use a "
			a href="#customized-built-in-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:customized-built-in-element-7" {
				- "customized built-in element"
			}
			- " as an "
			a href="#autonomous-custom-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:autonomous-custom-element-4" {
				- "autonomous custom\n  element"
			}
			- " will "
			em {
				- "not"
			}
			- " work; that is, "
			code {
				- "<plastic-button>Click\n  me?</plastic-button>"
			}
			- " will simply create an "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:htmlelement-2" {
				a href="#htmlelement" {
					- "HTMLElement"
				}
			}
			- " with no special\n  behavior."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "If you need to create a customized built-in element programmatically, you can use the following\n  form of "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:dom-document-createelement" {
				a data-x-internal="dom-document-createelement" href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-document-createelement" {
					- "createElement()"
				}
			}
			- ":"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="js" {
				c- a="" {
					- "const"
				}
				- " plasticButton "
				c- o="" {
					- "="
				}
				- " document"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "createElement"
				c- p="" {
					- "("
				}
				c- u="" {
					- "\"button\""
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ","
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "{"
				}
				- " is"
				c- o="" {
					- ":"
				}
				- " "
				c- u="" {
					- "\"plastic-button\""
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "});"
				}
				- "\nplasticButton"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "textContent "
				c- o="" {
					- "="
				}
				- " "
				c- u="" {
					- "\"Click me!\""
				}
				c- p="" {
					- ";"
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "And as before, the constructor will also work:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="js" {
				c- a="" {
					- "const"
				}
				- " plasticButton2 "
				c- o="" {
					- "="
				}
				- " "
				c- k="" {
					- "new"
				}
				- " PlasticButton"
				c- p="" {
					- "();"
				}
				- "\nconsole"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "log"
				c- p="" {
					- "("
				}
				- "plasticButton2"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "localName"
				c- p="" {
					- ");"
				}
				- "  "
				c- c1="" {
					- "// will output \"button\""
				}
				- "\nconsole"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "assert"
				c- p="" {
					- "("
				}
				- "plasticButton2 "
				c- k="" {
					- "instanceof"
				}
				- " PlasticButton"
				c- p="" {
					- ");"
				}
				- "\nconsole"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "assert"
				c- p="" {
					- "("
				}
				- "plasticButton2 "
				c- k="" {
					- "instanceof"
				}
				- " HTMLButtonElement"
				c- p="" {
					- ");"
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Note that when creating a customized built-in element programmatically, the "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:attr-is-2" {
				a href="#attr-is" {
					- "is"
				}
			}
			- " attribute will not be present in the DOM, since it was not explicitly\n  set. However, "
			a href="#attr-is-during-serialization" {
				- "it will be added to the output when\n  serializing"
			}
			- ":"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="js" {
				- "console"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "assert"
				c- p="" {
					- "("
				}
				c- o="" {
					- "!"
				}
				- "plasticButton"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "hasAttribute"
				c- p="" {
					- "("
				}
				c- u="" {
					- "\"is\""
				}
				c- p="" {
					- "));"
				}
				- "\nconsole"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "log"
				c- p="" {
					- "("
				}
				- "plasticButton"
				c- p="" {
					- "."
				}
				- "outerHTML"
				c- p="" {
					- ");"
				}
				- " "
				c- c1="" {
					- "// will output '<button is=\"plastic-button\"></button>'"
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Regardless of how it is created, all of the ways in which "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:the-button-element-2" {
				a href="#the-button-element" {
					- "button"
				}
			}
			- " is special\n  apply to such \"plastic buttons\" as well: their focus behavior, ability to participate in "
			a href="#concept-form-submit" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:concept-form-submit" {
				- "form submission"
			}
			- ", the "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:attr-fe-disabled" {
				a href="#attr-fe-disabled" {
					- "disabled"
				}
			}
			- " attribute, and so on."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p id="customized-built-in-element-restrictions" {
			a href="#customized-built-in-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:customized-built-in-element-8" {
				- "Customized built-in elements"
			}
			- " are designed to allow extension of existing HTML\n  elements that have useful user-agent supplied behavior or APIs. As such, they can only extend\n  existing HTML elements defined in this specification, and cannot extend legacy elements such as\n  "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:bgsound" {
				a href="#bgsound" {
					- "bgsound"
				}
			}
			- ", "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:blink" {
				a href="#blink" {
					- "blink"
				}
			}
			- ", "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:isindex" {
				a href="#isindex" {
					- "isindex"
				}
			}
			- ", "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:keygen" {
				a href="#keygen" {
					- "keygen"
				}
			}
			- ",\n  "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:multicol" {
				a href="#multicol" {
					- "multicol"
				}
			}
			- ", "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:nextid" {
				a href="#nextid" {
					- "nextid"
				}
			}
			- ", or "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:spacer" {
				a href="#spacer" {
					- "spacer"
				}
			}
			- " that have been defined to use\n  "
			code id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:htmlunknownelement" {
				a href="#htmlunknownelement" {
					- "HTMLUnknownElement"
				}
			}
			- " as their "
			a id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:element-interface" href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-element-interface" data-x-internal="element-interface" {
				- "element interface"
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "One reason for this requirement is future-compatibility: if a "
			a href="#customized-built-in-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:customized-built-in-element-9" {
				- "customized built-in\n  element"
			}
			- " was defined that extended a currently-unknown element, for example "
			code {
				- "combobox"
			}
			- ", this would prevent this specification from defining a "
			code {
				- "combobox"
			}
			- " element in the future, as consumers of the derived "
			a href="#customized-built-in-element" id="custom-elements-customized-builtin-example:customized-built-in-element-10" {
				- "customized\n  built-in element"
			}
			- " would have come to depend on their base element having no interesting\n  user-agent-supplied behavior."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		h5 id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.13.1.5"
			}
			- " Drawbacks of autonomous custom elements"
			a href="#custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "As specified below, and alluded to above, simply defining and using an element called\n  "
			code {
				- "taco-button"
			}
			- " does not mean that such elements "
			a href="#represents" id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:represents" {
				- "represent"
			}
			- " buttons. That is, tools such as web browsers, search engines,\n  or accessibility technology will not automatically treat the resulting element as a button just\n  based on its defined name."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "To convey the desired button semantics to a variety of users, while still using an\n  "
			a href="#autonomous-custom-element" id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:autonomous-custom-element" {
				- "autonomous custom element"
			}
			- ", a number of techniques would need to be employed:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					- "The addition of the "
					code id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:attr-tabindex" {
						a href="#attr-tabindex" {
							- "tabindex"
						}
					}
					- " attribute would make the\n   "
					code {
						- "taco-button"
					}
					- " "
					a href="#focusable" id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:focusable" {
						- "focusable"
					}
					- ". Note that if the\n   "
					code {
						- "taco-button"
					}
					- " were to become logically disabled, the "
					code id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:attr-tabindex-2" {
						a href="#attr-tabindex" {
							- "tabindex"
						}
					}
					- " attribute would need to be removed."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "The addition of an ARIA role and various ARIA states and properties helps convey semantics\n   to accessibility technology. For example, setting the "
					a id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:role" href="https://w3c.github.io/aria/#dfn-role" data-x-internal="role" {
						- "role"
					}
					- " to \""
					code id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:attr-aria-role-button" {
						a data-x-internal="attr-aria-role-button" href="https://w3c.github.io/aria/#button" {
							- "button"
						}
					}
					- "\" will convey the semantics that this is a button,\n   enabling users to successfully interact with the control using usual button-like interactions in\n   their accessibility technology. Setting the "
					code id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:attr-aria-label" {
						a data-x-internal="attr-aria-label" href="https://w3c.github.io/aria/#aria-label" {
							- "aria-label"
						}
					}
					- "\n   property is necessary to give the button an "
					a href="https://w3c.github.io/aria/#dfn-accessible-name" id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:concept-accessible-name" data-x-internal="concept-accessible-name" {
						- "accessible\n   name"
					}
					- ", instead of having accessibility technology traverse its child text nodes and\n   announce them. And setting the "
					code id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:attr-aria-disabled" {
						a data-x-internal="attr-aria-disabled" href="https://w3c.github.io/aria/#aria-disabled" {
							- "aria-disabled"
						}
					}
					- " state to\n   \""
					code {
						- "true"
					}
					- "\" when the button is logically disabled conveys to accessibility\n   technology the button's disabled state."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "The addition of event handlers to handle commonly-expected button behaviors helps convey\n   the semantics of the button to web browser users. In this case, the most relevant event handler\n   would be one that proxies appropriate "
					code id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:event-keydown" {
						a data-x-internal="event-keydown" href="https://w3c.github.io/uievents/#event-type-keydown" {
							- "keydown"
						}
					}
					- " events to\n   become "
					code id="custom-elements-autonomous-drawbacks:event-click" {
						a data-x-internal="event-click" href="https://w3c.github.io/uievents/#event-type-click" {
							- "click"
						}
					}
					- " events, so that you can activate the button both\n   with keyboard and by clicking."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "In addition to any default visual styling provided for "
					code {
						- "taco-button"
					}
					- "\n   elements, the visual styling will also need to be updated to reflect changes in logical state,\n   such as becoming disabled; that is, whatever style sheet has rules for "
					code {
						- "taco-button"
					}
					- " will also need to have rules for "
					code {
						- "taco-button[disabled]"
					}
					- "."
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "With these points in mind, a full-featured "
			code {
				- "taco-button"
			}
			- " that took on the\n  responsibility of conveying button semantics (including the ability to be disabled) might look\n  something like this:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		pre {
			code class="js" {
				c- a="" {
					- "class"
				}
				- " TacoButton "
				c- k="" {
					- "extends"
				}
				- " HTMLElement "
				c- p="" {
					- "{"
				}
				- "\n  "
				c- k="" {
					- "static"
				}
				- " observedAttributes "
				c- o="" {
					- "="
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "["
				}
				c- u="" {
					- "\"disabled\""
				}
				c- p="" {
					- "];"
				}
				- "\n\n  constructor"
				c- p="" {
					- "()"
				}
				- " "
				c- p="" {
					- "{"
				}
				- "\n    "
				c- k="" {
					- "super"
