		- "\n\n  "
		h5 id="exposing-custom-element-states" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.13.1.7"
			}
			- " Exposing custom element states"
			a href="#exposing-custom-element-states" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Built-in elements provided by user agents have certain states that can change over time\n  depending on user interaction and other factors, and are exposed to web authors through "
			a href="https://drafts.csswg.org/selectors/#pseudo-class" id="exposing-custom-element-states:pseudo-class" data-x-internal="pseudo-class" {
				- "pseudo-classes"
			}
			- ". For example, some form controls have the \"invalid\"\n  state, which is exposed through the "
			code id="exposing-custom-element-states:selector-invalid" {
				a href="#selector-invalid" {
					- ":invalid"
				}
			}
			- "\n  "
			a id="exposing-custom-element-states:pseudo-class-2" href="https://drafts.csswg.org/selectors/#pseudo-class" data-x-internal="pseudo-class" {
				- "pseudo-class"
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Like built-in elements, "
			a href="#custom-element" id="exposing-custom-element-states:custom-element" {
				- "custom elements"
			}
			- " can have various\n  states to be in too, and "
			a href="#custom-element" id="exposing-custom-element-states:custom-element-2" {
				- "custom element"
			}
			- " authors want to expose these states in a\n  similar fashion as the built-in elements."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This is done via the "
			code id="exposing-custom-element-states:selector-custom" {
				a href="#selector-custom" {
					- ":state()"
				}
			}
			- " pseudo-class. A custom\n  element author can use the "
			code id="exposing-custom-element-states:dom-elementinternals-states" {
				a href="#dom-elementinternals-states" {
					- "states"
				}
			}
			- " property of\n  "
			code id="exposing-custom-element-states:elementinternals" {
				a href="#elementinternals" {
					- "ElementInternals"
				}
			}
			- " to add and remove such custom states, which are then exposed as\n  arguments to the "
			code id="exposing-custom-element-states:selector-custom-2" {
				a href="#selector-custom" {
					- ":state()"
				}
			}
			- " pseudo-class.\n\n  "
		}
