			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "The actual frequency of each tag is given using the "
				code id="tag-clouds:attr-title" {
					a href="#attr-title" {
						- "title"
					}
				}
				- "\n   attribute. A CSS style sheet is provided to convert the markup into a cloud of differently-sized\n   words, but for user agents that do not support CSS or are not visual, the markup contains\n   annotations like \"(popular)\" or \"(rare)\" to categorize the various tags by frequency, thus\n   enabling all users to benefit from the information."
			}
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "The "
				code id="tag-clouds:the-ul-element-2" {
					a href="#the-ul-element" {
						- "ul"
					}
				}
				- " element is used (rather than "
				code id="tag-clouds:the-ol-element" {
					a href="#the-ol-element" {
						- "ol"
					}
				}
				- ") because the order is not\n   particularly important: while the list is in fact ordered alphabetically, it would convey the\n   same information if ordered by, say, the length of the tag."
			}
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "The "
				code id="tag-clouds:link-type-tag" {
					a href="#link-type-tag" {
						- "tag"
					}
				}
				- " "
				code id="tag-clouds:attr-hyperlink-rel" {
					a href="#attr-hyperlink-rel" {
						- "rel"
					}
				}
				- "-keyword is\n   "
				em {
					- "not"
				}
				- " used on these "
				code id="tag-clouds:the-a-element" {
					a href="#the-a-element" {
						- "a"
					}
				}
				- " elements because they do not represent tags that apply\n   to the page itself; they are just part of an index listing the tags themselves."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n\n  "
		h4 id="conversations" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "4.14.3"
			}
			- " Conversations"
			a href="#conversations" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		p {
			- "This specification does not define a specific element for marking up conversations, meeting\n  minutes, chat transcripts, dialogues in screenplays, instant message logs, and other situations\n  where different players take turns in discourse."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Instead, authors are encouraged to mark up conversations using "
			code id="conversations:the-p-element" {
				a href="#the-p-element" {
					- "p"
				}
			}
			- " elements and\n  punctuation. Authors who need to mark the speaker for styling purposes are encouraged to use\n  "
			code id="conversations:the-span-element" {
				a href="#the-span-element" {
					- "span"
				}
			}
			- " or "
			code id="conversations:the-b-element" {
				a href="#the-b-element" {
					- "b"
				}
			}
			- ". Paragraphs with their text wrapped in the "
			code id="conversations:the-i-element" {
				a href="#the-i-element" {
					- "i"
				}
			}
			- "\n  element can be used for marking up stage directions."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		div class="example" {
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "This example demonstrates this using an extract from Abbot and Costello's famous sketch,\n   "
				cite {
					- "Who's on first"
				}
				- ":"
			}
			- "\n\n"
			pre {
				code class="html" {
					c- p="" {
						- "<"
					}
					c- f="" {
						- "p"
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ">"
					}
					- " Costello: Look, you gotta first baseman?\n"
					c- p="" {
						- "<"
					}
					c- f="" {
						- "p"
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ">"
					}
					- " Abbott: Certainly.\n"
					c- p="" {
						- "<"
					}
					c- f="" {
						- "p"
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ">"
					}
					- " Costello: Who's playing first?\n"
					c- p="" {
						- "<"
					}
					c- f="" {
						- "p"
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ">"
					}
					- " Abbott: That's right.\n"
					c- p="" {
						- "<"
