		- "\n\n  \n\n\n\n  \n\n  "
		h4 id="security-risks-in-the-drag-and-drop-model" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "6.11.8"
			}
			- " Security risks in the drag-and-drop model"
			a href="#security-risks-in-the-drag-and-drop-model" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "User agents must not make the data added to the "
			code id="security-risks-in-the-drag-and-drop-model:datatransfer" {
				a href="#datatransfer" {
					- "DataTransfer"
				}
			}
			- " object during the\n  "
			code id="security-risks-in-the-drag-and-drop-model:event-dnd-dragstart" {
				a href="#event-dnd-dragstart" {
					- "dragstart"
				}
			}
			- " event available to scripts until the "
			code id="security-risks-in-the-drag-and-drop-model:event-dnd-drop" {
				a href="#event-dnd-drop" {
					- "drop"
				}
			}
			- " event, because otherwise, if a user were to drag sensitive\n  information from one document to a second document, crossing a hostile third document in the\n  process, the hostile document could intercept the data."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "For the same reason, user agents must consider a drop to be successful only if the user\n  specifically ended the drag operation — if any scripts end the drag operation, it must be\n  considered unsuccessful (canceled) and the "
			code id="security-risks-in-the-drag-and-drop-model:event-dnd-drop-2" {
				a href="#event-dnd-drop" {
					- "drop"
				}
			}
			- " event must not be\n  fired."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "User agents should take care to not start drag-and-drop operations in response to script\n  actions. For example, in a mouse-and-window environment, if a script moves a window while the user\n  has their mouse button depressed, the UA would not consider that to start a drag. This is important\n  because otherwise UAs could cause data to be dragged from sensitive sources and dropped into\n  hostile documents without the user's consent."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "User agents should filter potentially active (scripted) content (e.g. HTML) when it is dragged\n  and when it is dropped, using a safelist of known-safe features. Similarly, "
			a href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#syntax-url-relative" id="security-risks-in-the-drag-and-drop-model:relative-url" data-x-internal="relative-url" {
				- "relative URLs"
			}
			- " should be turned into absolute URLs to avoid references changing in\n  unexpected ways. This specification does not specify how this is performed."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		div class="example" {
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "Consider a hostile page providing some content and getting the user to select and drag and\n   drop (or indeed, copy and paste) that content to a victim page's "
				code id="security-risks-in-the-drag-and-drop-model:attr-contenteditable" {
					a href="#attr-contenteditable" {
						- "contenteditable"
					}
				}
				- " region. If the browser does not ensure that\n   only safe content is dragged, potentially unsafe content such as scripts and event handlers in\n   the selection, once dropped (or pasted) into the victim site, get the privileges of the victim\n   site. This would thus enable a cross-site scripting attack."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		h3 id="the-popover-attribute" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "6.12"
