			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "The key difference is that when using "
				code id="examples-5:dom-eventtarget-addeventlistener" {
					a data-x-internal="dom-eventtarget-addeventlistener" href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-eventtarget-addeventlistener" {
						- "addEventListener()"
					}
				}
				- ", the "
				code id="examples-5:dom-messageport-start" {
					a href="#dom-messageport-start" {
						- "start()"
					}
				}
				- " method must also be invoked. When using "
				code id="examples-5:handler-messageeventtarget-onmessage" {
					a href="#handler-messageeventtarget-onmessage" {
						- "onmessage"
					}
				}
				- ", the call to "
				code id="examples-5:dom-messageport-start-2" {
					a href="#dom-messageport-start" {
						- "start()"
					}
				}
				- " is implied."
			}
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "The "
				code id="examples-5:dom-messageport-start-3" {
					a href="#dom-messageport-start" {
						- "start()"
					}
				}
				- " method, whether called explicitly or\n   implicitly (by setting "
				code id="examples-5:handler-messageeventtarget-onmessage-2" {
					a href="#handler-messageeventtarget-onmessage" {
						- "onmessage"
					}
				}
				- "),\n   starts the flow of messages: messages posted on message ports are initially paused, so that they\n   don't get dropped on the floor before the script has had a chance to set up its handlers."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n\n  "
		h5 id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "9.4.1.2"
			}
			- " Ports as the basis of an object-capability model on the web"
			a href="#ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Ports can be viewed as a way to expose limited capabilities (in the object-capability model\n  sense) to other actors in the system. This can either be a weak capability system, where the ports\n  are merely used as a convenient model within a particular origin, or as a strong capability model,\n  where they are provided by one origin "
			var {
				- "provider"
			}
			- " as the only mechanism by which\n  another origin "
			var {
				- "consumer"
			}
			- " can effect change in or obtain information from "
			var {
				- "provider"
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "For example, consider a situation in which a social web site embeds in one "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:the-iframe-element" {
				a href="#the-iframe-element" {
					- "iframe"
				}
			}
			- "\n  the user's email contacts provider (an address book site, from a second origin), and in a second\n  "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:the-iframe-element-2" {
				a href="#the-iframe-element" {
					- "iframe"
				}
			}
			- " a game (from a third origin). The outer social site and the game in the second\n  "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:the-iframe-element-3" {
				a href="#the-iframe-element" {
					- "iframe"
				}
			}
			- " cannot access anything inside the first "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:the-iframe-element-4" {
				a href="#the-iframe-element" {
					- "iframe"
				}
			}
			- "; together they can\n  only:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					a href="#navigate" id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:navigate" {
						- "Navigate"
					}
					- " the "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:the-iframe-element-5" {
						a href="#the-iframe-element" {
							- "iframe"
						}
					}
					- " to a new "
					a id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:url" href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" data-x-internal="url" {
						- "URL"
					}
					- ", such as the same\n   "
					a id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:url-2" href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" data-x-internal="url" {
						- "URL"
					}
					- " but with a different "
					a href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url-fragment" id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:concept-url-fragment" data-x-internal="concept-url-fragment" {
						- "fragment"
					}
					- ",\n   causing the "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:window" {
						a href="#window" {
							- "Window"
						}
					}
					- " in the "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:the-iframe-element-6" {
						a href="#the-iframe-element" {
							- "iframe"
						}
					}
					- " to receive a "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:event-hashchange" {
						a href="#event-hashchange" {
							- "hashchange"
						}
					}
					- " event."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Resize the "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:the-iframe-element-7" {
						a href="#the-iframe-element" {
							- "iframe"
						}
					}
					- ", causing the "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:window-2" {
						a href="#window" {
							- "Window"
						}
					}
					- " in the "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:the-iframe-element-8" {
						a href="#the-iframe-element" {
							- "iframe"
						}
					}
					- "\n   to receive a "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:event-resize" {
						a data-x-internal="event-resize" href="https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view/#eventdef-window-resize" {
							- "resize"
						}
					}
					- " event."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Send a "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:event-message" {
						a href="#event-message" {
							- "message"
						}
					}
					- " event to the "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:window-3" {
						a href="#window" {
							- "Window"
						}
					}
					- " in the\n   "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:the-iframe-element-9" {
						a href="#the-iframe-element" {
							- "iframe"
						}
					}
					- " using the "
					code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:dom-window-postmessage" {
						a href="#dom-window-postmessage" {
							- "window.postMessage()"
						}
					}
					- "\n   API."
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The contacts provider can use these methods, most particularly the third one, to provide an API\n  that can be accessed by other origins to manipulate the user's address book. For example, it could\n  respond to a message \""
			code {
				- "add-contact Guillaume Tell\n  <tell@pomme.example.net>"
			}
			- "\" by adding the given person and email address to the user's\n  address book."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "To avoid any site on the web being able to manipulate the user's contacts, the contacts\n  provider might only allow certain trusted sites, such as the social site, to do this."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Now suppose the game wanted to add a contact to the user's address book, and that the social\n  site was willing to allow it to do so on its behalf, essentially \"sharing\" the trust that the\n  contacts provider had with the social site. There are several ways it could do this; most simply,\n  it could just proxy messages between the game site and the contacts site. However, this solution\n  has a number of difficulties: it requires the social site to either completely trust the game site\n  not to abuse the privilege, or it requires that the social site verify each request to make sure\n  it's not a request that it doesn't want to allow (such as adding multiple contacts, reading the\n  contacts, or deleting them); it also requires some additional complexity if there's ever the\n  possibility of multiple games simultaneously trying to interact with the contacts provider."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Using message channels and "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:messageport" {
				a href="#messageport" {
					- "MessagePort"
				}
			}
			- " objects, however, all of these problems can\n  go away. When the game tells the social site that it wants to add a contact, the social site can\n  ask the contacts provider not for it to add a contact, but for the "
			em {
				- "capability"
			}
			- " to add a\n  single contact. The contacts provider then creates a pair of "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-an-object-capability-model-on-the-web:messageport-2" {
				a href="#messageport" {
					- "MessagePort"
				}
			}
			- " objects, and\n  sends one of them back to the social site, who forwards it on to the game. The game and the\n  contacts provider then have a direct connection, and the contacts provider knows to only honor a\n  single \"add contact\" request, nothing else. In other words, the game has been granted the\n  capability to add a single contact."
		}
		- "\n\n\n  "
		h5 id="ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "9.4.1.3"
			}
			- " Ports as the basis of abstracting out service implementations"
			a href="#ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Continuing the example from the previous section, consider the contacts provider in particular.\n  While an initial implementation might have simply used "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations:xmlhttprequest" {
				a data-x-internal="xmlhttprequest" href="https://xhr.spec.whatwg.org/#xmlhttprequest" {
					- "XMLHttpRequest"
				}
			}
			- " objects in the\n  service's "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations:the-iframe-element" {
				a href="#the-iframe-element" {
					- "iframe"
				}
			}
			- ", an evolution of the service might instead want to use a "
			a href="#sharedworker" id="ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations:sharedworker" {
				- "shared worker"
			}
			- " with a single "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations:websocket-2" {
				a data-x-internal="websocket-2" href="https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#websocket" {
					- "WebSocket"
				}
			}
			- " connection."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "If the initial design used "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations:messageport" {
				a href="#messageport" {
					- "MessagePort"
				}
			}
			- " objects to grant capabilities, or even just\n  to allow multiple simultaneous independent sessions, the service implementation can switch from\n  the "
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations:xmlhttprequest-2" {
				a data-x-internal="xmlhttprequest" href="https://xhr.spec.whatwg.org/#xmlhttprequest" {
					- "XMLHttpRequest"
				}
			}
			- "s-in-each-"
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations:the-iframe-element-2" {
				a href="#the-iframe-element" {
					- "iframe"
				}
			}
			- " model to the\n  shared-"
			code id="ports-as-the-basis-of-abstracting-out-service-implementations:websocket-2-2" {
				a data-x-internal="websocket-2" href="https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#websocket" {
					- "WebSocket"
				}
			}
			- " model without changing the API at all: the ports on the service\n  provider side can all be forwarded to the shared worker without it affecting the users of the API\n  in the slightest."
		}
		- "\n\n\n\n  "
