		- "\n  \n\n  \n\n  "
		div class="note" {
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "Thus, a message port can be received, given an event listener, and then forgotten, and so long\n   as that event listener could receive a message, the channel will be maintained."
			}
			- "\n\n   "
			p {
				- "Of course, if this was to occur on both sides of the channel, then both ports could be garbage\n   collected, since they would not be reachable from live code, despite having a strong reference to\n   each other. However, if a message port has a pending message, it is not garbage collected."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
		}
		- "\n\n  \n\n  "
		p class="note" {
			- "Authors are strongly encouraged to explicitly close "
			code id="ports-and-garbage-collection:messageport-7" {
				a href="#messageport" {
					- "MessagePort"
				}
			}
			- "\n  objects to disentangle them, so that their resources can be recollected. Creating many\n  "
			code id="ports-and-garbage-collection:messageport-8" {
				a href="#messageport" {
					- "MessagePort"
				}
			}
			- " objects and discarding them without closing them can lead to high\n  transient memory usage since garbage collection is not necessarily performed promptly, especially\n  for "
			code id="ports-and-garbage-collection:messageport-9" {
				a href="#messageport" {
					- "MessagePort"
				}
			}
			- "s where garbage collection can involve cross-process coordination."
		}
