		- "\n\n  \n\n\n  "
		h2 id="worklets" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "11"
			}
			- " Worklets"
			a href="#worklets" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		h3 id="worklets-intro" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "11.1"
			}
			- " Introduction"
			a href="#worklets-intro" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Worklets are a piece of specification infrastructure which can be used for running scripts\n  independent of the main JavaScript execution environment, while not requiring any particular\n  implementation model."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The worklet infrastructure specified here cannot be used directly by web developers. Instead,\n  other specifications build upon it to create directly-usable worklet types, specialized for\n  running in particular parts of the browser implementation pipeline."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		h4 id="worklets-motivations" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "11.1.1"
			}
			- " Motivations"
			a href="#worklets-motivations" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			i {
				- "This section is non-normative."
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Allowing extension points to rendering, or other sensitive parts of the implementation pipeline\n  such as audio output, is difficult. If extension points were done with full access to the APIs\n  available on "
			code id="worklets-motivations:window" {
				a href="#window" {
					- "Window"
				}
			}
			- ", engines would need to abandon previously-held assumptions for\n  what could happen in the middle of those phases. For example, during the layout phase, rendering\n  engines assume that no DOM will be modified."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Additionally, defining extension points in the "
			code id="worklets-motivations:window-2" {
				a href="#window" {
					- "Window"
				}
			}
			- " environment would restrict\n  user agents to performing work in the same thread as the "
			code id="worklets-motivations:window-3" {
				a href="#window" {
					- "Window"
				}
			}
			- " object. (Unless\n  implementations added complex, high-overhead infrastructure to allow thread-safe APIs, as well\n  as thread-joining guarantees.)"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Worklets are designed to allow extension points, while keeping guarantees that user agents\n  currently rely on. This is done through new global environments, based on subclasses of\n  "
			code id="worklets-motivations:workletglobalscope" {
				a href="#workletglobalscope" {
					- "WorkletGlobalScope"
				}
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Worklets are similar to web workers. However, they:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					- "Are thread-agnostic. That is, they are not designed to run on a dedicated separate thread,\n   like each worker is. Implementations can run worklets wherever they choose (including on the main\n   thread)."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Are able to have multiple duplicate instances of the global scope created, for the purpose\n   of parallelism."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Do not use an event-based API. Instead, classes are registered on the global scope, whose\n   methods are invoked by the user agent."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Have a reduced API surface on the global scope."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Have a lifetime for their "
					a href="#global-object" id="worklets-motivations:global-object" {
						- "global object"
					}
					- " which is defined by other\n   specifications, often in an "
					a id="worklets-motivations:implementation-defined" href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation-defined" data-x-internal="implementation-defined" {
						- "implementation-defined"
					}
					- " manner."
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "As worklets have relatively high overhead, they are best used sparingly. Due to this, a given\n  "
			code id="worklets-motivations:workletglobalscope-2" {
				a href="#workletglobalscope" {
					- "WorkletGlobalScope"
				}
			}
			- " is expected to be shared between multiple separate scripts. (This\n  is similar to how a single "
			code id="worklets-motivations:window-4" {
				a href="#window" {
					- "Window"
				}
			}
			- " is shared between multiple separate scripts.)"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Worklets are a general technology that serve different use cases. Some worklets, such as those\n  defined in "
			cite {
				- "CSS Painting API"
			}
			- ", provide extension points intended for stateless,\n  idempotent, and short-running computations, which have special considerations as described in the\n  next couple of sections. Others, such as those defined in "
			cite {
				- "Web Audio API"
			}
			- ", are used for\n  stateful, long-running operations. "
			a href="#refsCSSPAINT" {
				- "[CSSPAINT]"
			}
			- " "
			a href="#refsWEBAUDIO" {
				- "[WEBAUDIO]"
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		h4 id="worklets-idempotent" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "11.1.2"
			}
			- " Code idempotence"
			a href="#worklets-idempotent" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Some specifications which use worklets are intended to allow user agents to parallelize work\n  over multiple threads, or to move work between threads as required. In these specifications, user\n  agents might invoke methods on a web-developer-provided class in an\n  "
			a id="worklets-idempotent:implementation-defined" href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation-defined" data-x-internal="implementation-defined" {
				- "implementation-defined"
			}
			- " order."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "As a result of this, to prevent interoperability issues, authors who register classes on such\n  "
			code id="worklets-idempotent:workletglobalscope" {
				a href="#workletglobalscope" {
					- "WorkletGlobalScope"
				}
			}
			- "s should make their code idempotent. That is, a method or set of\n  methods on the class should produce the same output given a particular input."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "This specification uses the following techniques in order to encourage authors to write code in\n  an idempotent way:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		ul {
			li {
				- "\n    "
				p {
					- "No reference to the global object is available (i.e., there is no counterpart to "
					code id="worklets-idempotent:dom-workerglobalscope-self" {
						a href="#dom-workerglobalscope-self" {
							- "self"
						}
					}
					- " on "
					code id="worklets-idempotent:workletglobalscope-2" {
						a href="#workletglobalscope" {
							- "WorkletGlobalScope"
						}
					}
					- ")."
				}
				- "\n\n    "
				p class="XXX" {
					- "Although this was the intention when worklets were first specified, the\n    introduction of "
					code {
						- "globalThis"
					}
					- " has made it no longer true. See "
					a href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/6059" {
						- "issue #6059"
					}
					- " for more discussion."
				}
				- "\n   "
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Code is loaded as a "
					a href="#module-script" id="worklets-idempotent:module-script" {
						- "module script"
					}
					- ", which results in the code being executed\n   in strict mode and with no shared "
					code {
						- "this"
					}
					- " referencing the global\n   proxy."
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Together, these restrictions help prevent two different scripts from sharing state using\n  properties of the "
			a href="#global-object" id="worklets-idempotent:global-object" {
				- "global object"
			}
			- "."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Additionally, specifications which use worklets and intend to allow\n  "
			a id="worklets-idempotent:implementation-defined-2" href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation-defined" data-x-internal="implementation-defined" {
				- "implementation-defined"
			}
			- " behavior must obey the following:"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					- "They must require user agents to always have at least two "
					code id="worklets-idempotent:workletglobalscope-3" {
						a href="#workletglobalscope" {
							- "WorkletGlobalScope"
						}
					}
					- "\n   instances per "
					code id="worklets-idempotent:worklet" {
						a href="#worklet" {
							- "Worklet"
						}
					}
					- ", and randomly assign a method or set of methods on a class to\n   a particular "
					code id="worklets-idempotent:workletglobalscope-4" {
						a href="#workletglobalscope" {
							- "WorkletGlobalScope"
						}
					}
					- " instance. These specifications may provide an\n   opt-out under memory constraints."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "These specifications must allow user agents to create and destroy instances of their\n   "
					code id="worklets-idempotent:workletglobalscope-5" {
						a href="#workletglobalscope" {
							- "WorkletGlobalScope"
						}
					}
					- " subclasses at any time."
				}
			}
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		h4 id="worklets-speculative" {
			span class="secno" {
				- "11.1.3"
			}
			- " Speculative evaluation"
			a href="#worklets-speculative" class="self-link"
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "Some specifications which use worklets can invoke methods on a web-developer-provided class\n  based on the state of the user agent. To increase concurrency between threads, a user agent may\n  invoke a method speculatively, based on potential future states."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "In these specifications, user agents might invoke such methods at any time, and with any\n  arguments, not just ones corresponding to the current state of the user agent. The results of such\n  speculative evaluations are not displayed immediately, but can be cached for use if the user agent\n  state matches the speculated state. This can increase the concurrency between the user agent and\n  worklet threads."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "As a result of this, to prevent interoperability risks between user agents, authors who\n  register classes on such "
			code id="worklets-speculative:workletglobalscope" {
				a href="#workletglobalscope" {
					- "WorkletGlobalScope"
				}
			}
			- "s should make their code stateless. That\n  is, the only effect of invoking a method should be its result, and not any side effects such as\n  updating mutable state."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		p {
			- "The same techniques which encourage "
			a href="#worklets-idempotent" {
				- "code idempotence"
			}
			- " also\n  encourage authors to write stateless code."
		}
		- "\n\n  "
		div class="non-normative" {
			- "\n\n  "
			h3 id="worklets-examples" {
				span class="secno" {
					- "11.2"
				}
				- " Examples"
				a href="#worklets-examples" class="self-link"
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				i {
					- "This section is non-normative."
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "For these examples, we'll use a fake worklet. The "
				code id="worklets-examples:window" {
					a href="#window" {
						- "Window"
					}
				}
				- " object provides two\n  "
				code id="worklets-examples:worklet" {
					a href="#worklet" {
						- "Worklet"
					}
				}
				- " instances, which each run code in their own collection of\n  "
				code id="worklets-examples:fakeworkletglobalscope" {
					a href="#fakeworkletglobalscope" {
						- "FakeWorkletGlobalScope"
					}
				}
				- "s:\n\n  "
			}
			pre class="extract" {
				code class="idl" {
					c- b="" {
						- "partial"
					}
					- " "
					c- b="" {
						- "interface"
					}
					- " "
					a href="#window" id="worklets-examples:window-2" {
						c- g="" {
							- "Window"
						}
					}
					- " {\n  ["
					c- g="" {
						- "SameObject"
					}
					- ", "
					c- g="" {
						- "SecureContext"
					}
					- "] "
					c- b="" {
						- "readonly"
					}
					- " "
					c- b="" {
						- "attribute"
					}
					- " "
					a href="#worklet" id="worklets-examples:worklet-2" {
						c- n="" {
							- "Worklet"
						}
					}
					- " "
					a href="#fakeworklet1" id="worklets-examples:fakeworklet1" {
						c- g="" {
							- "fakeWorklet1"
						}
					}
					- ";\n  ["
					c- g="" {
						- "SameObject"
					}
					- ", "
					c- g="" {
						- "SecureContext"
					}
					- "] "
					c- b="" {
						- "readonly"
					}
					- " "
					c- b="" {
						- "attribute"
					}
					- " "
					a href="#worklet" id="worklets-examples:worklet-3" {
						c- n="" {
							- "Worklet"
						}
					}
					- " "
					a href="#fakeworklet2" id="worklets-examples:fakeworklet2" {
						c- g="" {
							- "fakeWorklet2"
						}
					}
					- ";\n};"
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  \n\n  "
			p {
				- "Each "
				code id="worklets-examples:window-3" {
					a href="#window" {
						- "Window"
					}
				}
				- " has two "
				code id="worklets-examples:worklet-4" {
					a href="#worklet" {
						- "Worklet"
					}
				}
				- " instances, "
				dfn id="fake-worklet-1" {
					- "fake\n  worklet 1"
				}
				- " and "
				dfn id="fake-worklet-2" {
					- "fake worklet 2"
				}
				- ". Both of these have their "
				a href="#worklet-global-scope-type" id="worklets-examples:worklet-global-scope-type" {
					- "worklet\n  global scope type"
				}
				- " set to "
				code id="worklets-examples:fakeworkletglobalscope-2" {
					a href="#fakeworkletglobalscope" {
						- "FakeWorkletGlobalScope"
					}
				}
				- ", and their "
				a href="#worklet-destination-type" id="worklets-examples:worklet-destination-type" {
					- "worklet\n  destination type"
				}
				- " set to \""
				code {
					- "fakeworklet"
				}
				- "\". User agents should create at\n  least two "
				code id="worklets-examples:fakeworkletglobalscope-3" {
					a href="#fakeworkletglobalscope" {
						- "FakeWorkletGlobalScope"
					}
				}
				- " instances per worklet."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p class="note" {
				- "\""
				code {
					- "fakeworklet"
				}
				- "\" is not actually a valid "
				a href="https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-request-destination" id="worklets-examples:concept-request-destination" data-x-internal="concept-request-destination" {
					- "destination"
				}
				- " per "
				cite {
					- "Fetch"
				}
				- ". But this\n  illustrates how real worklets would generally have their own worklet-type-specific destination.\n  "
				a href="#refsFETCH" {
					- "[FETCH]"
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  \n\n  "
			p {
				- "The "
				dfn id="fakeworklet1" data-noexport="" {
					code {
						- "fakeWorklet1"
					}
				}
				- " getter steps are to return\n  "
				a id="worklets-examples:this" href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#this" data-x-internal="this" {
					- "this"
				}
				- "'s "
				a href="#fake-worklet-1" id="worklets-examples:fake-worklet-1" {
					- "fake worklet 1"
				}
				- "."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "The "
				dfn id="fakeworklet2" data-noexport="" {
					code {
						- "fakeWorklet2"
					}
				}
				- " getter steps are to return\n  "
				a id="worklets-examples:this-2" href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#this" data-x-internal="this" {
					- "this"
				}
				- "'s "
				a href="#fake-worklet-2" id="worklets-examples:fake-worklet-2" {
					- "fake worklet 2"
				}
				- "."
			}
			- "\n\n  \n\n  "
			hr
			- "\n\n  "
			pre class="extract" {
				code class="idl" {
					- "["
					c- g="" {
						- "Global"
					}
					- "=("
					c- n="" {
						- "Worklet"
					}
					- ","
					c- n="" {
						- "FakeWorklet"
					}
					- "),\n "
					c- g="" {
						- "Exposed"
					}
					- "="
					c- n="" {
						- "FakeWorklet"
					}
					- ",\n "
					c- g="" {
						- "SecureContext"
					}
					- "]\n"
					c- b="" {
						- "interface"
					}
					- " "
					dfn id="fakeworkletglobalscope" data-dfn-type="interface" data-noexport="" {
						c- g="" {
							- "FakeWorkletGlobalScope"
						}
					}
					- " : "
					a href="#workletglobalscope" id="worklets-examples:workletglobalscope" {
						c- n="" {
							- "WorkletGlobalScope"
						}
					}
					- " {\n  "
					c- b="" {
						- "undefined"
					}
					- " "
					a href="#dom-fakeworkletglobalscope-registerfake" id="worklets-examples:dom-fakeworkletglobalscope-registerfake" {
						c- g="" {
							- "registerFake"
						}
					}
					- "("
					c- b="" {
						- "DOMString"
					}
					- " "
					c- g="" {
						- "type"
					}
					- ", "
					a href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#common-Function" id="worklets-examples:idl-function" data-x-internal="idl-function" {
						c- n="" {
							- "Function"
						}
					}
					- " "
					c- g="" {
						- "classConstructor"
					}
					- ");\n};"
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  \n\n  \n\n  \n\n  "
			p {
				- "Each "
				code id="worklets-examples:fakeworkletglobalscope-4" {
					a href="#fakeworkletglobalscope" {
						- "FakeWorkletGlobalScope"
					}
				}
				- " has a "
				dfn id="registered-class-constructors-map" {
					- "registered class constructors map"
				}
				- ",\n  which is an "
				a id="worklets-examples:ordered-map" href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ordered-map" data-x-internal="ordered-map" {
					- "ordered map"
				}
				- ", initially empty."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "The "
				dfn id="dom-fakeworkletglobalscope-registerfake" data-noexport="" {
					code {
						- "registerFake("
						var {
							- "type"
						}
						- ", "
						var {
							- "classConstructor"
						}
						- ")"
					}
				}
				- " method\n  steps are to set "
				a id="worklets-examples:this-3" href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#this" data-x-internal="this" {
					- "this"
				}
				- "'s "
				a href="#registered-class-constructors-map" id="worklets-examples:registered-class-constructors-map" {
					- "registered class constructors\n  map"
				}
				- "["
				var {
					- "type"
				}
				- "] to "
				var {
					- "classConstructor"
				}
				- "."
			}
			- "\n\n  \n\n  "
			h4 id="worklets-examples-loading" {
				span class="secno" {
					- "11.2.1"
				}
				- " Loading scripts"
				a href="#worklets-examples-loading" class="self-link"
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				i {
					- "This section is non-normative."
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "To load scripts into "
				a href="#fake-worklet-1" id="worklets-examples-loading:fake-worklet-1" {
					- "fake worklet 1"
				}
				- ", a web developer would write:"
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			pre {
				code class="js" {
					- "window"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "fakeWorklet1"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "addModule"
					c- p="" {
						- "("
					}
					c- t="" {
						- "'script1.mjs'"
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ");"
					}
					- "\nwindow"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "fakeWorklet1"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "addModule"
					c- p="" {
						- "("
					}
					c- t="" {
						- "'script2.mjs'"
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ");"
					}
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "Note that which script finishes fetching and runs first is dependent on network timing: it\n  could be either "
				code {
					- "script1.mjs"
				}
				- " or "
				code {
					- "script2.mjs"
				}
				- ". This\n  generally won't matter for well-written scripts intended to be loaded in worklets, if they follow\n  the suggestions about preparing for "
				a href="#worklets-speculative" {
					- "speculative\n  evaluation"
				}
				- "."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "If a web developer wants to perform a task only after the scripts have successfully run and\n  loaded into some worklets, they could write:"
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			pre {
				code class="js" {
					- "Promise"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "all"
					c- p="" {
						- "(["
					}
					- "\n    window"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "fakeWorklet1"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "addModule"
					c- p="" {
						- "("
					}
					c- t="" {
						- "'script1.mjs'"
					}
					c- p="" {
						- "),"
					}
					- "\n    window"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "fakeWorklet2"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "addModule"
					c- p="" {
						- "("
					}
					c- t="" {
						- "'script2.mjs'"
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ")"
					}
					- "\n"
					c- p="" {
						- "])."
					}
					- "then"
					c- p="" {
						- "(()"
					}
					- " "
					c- p="" {
						- "=>"
					}
					- " "
					c- p="" {
						- "{"
					}
					- "\n    "
					c- c1="" {
						- "// Do something which relies on those scripts being loaded."
					}
					- "\n"
					c- p="" {
						- "});"
					}
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			hr
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "Another important point about script-loading is that loaded scripts can be run in multiple\n  "
				code id="worklets-examples-loading:workletglobalscope" {
					a href="#workletglobalscope" {
						- "WorkletGlobalScope"
					}
				}
				- "s per "
				code id="worklets-examples-loading:worklet" {
					a href="#worklet" {
						- "Worklet"
					}
				}
				- ", as discussed in the section on "
				a href="#worklets-idempotent" {
					- "code idempotence"
				}
				- ". In particular, the specification above\n  for "
				a href="#fake-worklet-1" id="worklets-examples-loading:fake-worklet-1-2" {
					- "fake worklet 1"
				}
				- " and "
				a href="#fake-worklet-2" id="worklets-examples-loading:fake-worklet-2" {
					- "fake worklet 2"
				}
				- " require this. So, consider a\n  scenario such as the following:"
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			pre {
				code class="js" {
					c- c1="" {
						- "// script.mjs"
					}
					- "\nconsole"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "log"
					c- p="" {
						- "("
					}
					c- u="" {
						- "\"Hello from a FakeWorkletGlobalScope!\""
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ");"
					}
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			pre {
				code class="js" {
					c- c1="" {
						- "// app.mjs"
					}
					- "\nwindow"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "fakeWorklet1"
					c- p="" {
						- "."
					}
					- "addModule"
					c- p="" {
						- "("
					}
					c- u="" {
						- "\"script.mjs\""
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ");"
					}
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "This could result in output such as the following from a user agent's console:"
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			pre {
				code {
					- "[fakeWorklet1#1] Hello from a FakeWorkletGlobalScope!\n[fakeWorklet1#4] Hello from a FakeWorkletGlobalScope!\n[fakeWorklet1#2] Hello from a FakeWorkletGlobalScope!\n[fakeWorklet1#3] Hello from a FakeWorkletGlobalScope!"
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "If the user agent at some point decided to kill and restart the third instance of\n  "
				code id="worklets-examples-loading:fakeworkletglobalscope" {
					a href="#fakeworkletglobalscope" {
						- "FakeWorkletGlobalScope"
					}
				}
				- ", the console would again print "
				code {
					- "[fakeWorklet1#3] Hello from a FakeWorkletGlobalScope!"
				}
				- " when this occurs."
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			h4 id="worklets-example-registering" {
				span class="secno" {
					- "11.2.2"
				}
				- " Registering a class and invoking its methods"
				a href="#worklets-example-registering" class="self-link"
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				i {
					- "This section is non-normative."
				}
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			p {
				- "Let's say that one of the intended usages of our fake worklet by web developers is to allow\n  them to customize the highly-complex process of boolean negation. They might register their\n  customization as follows:"
			}
			- "\n\n  "
			pre {
				code class="js" {
					c- c1="" {
						- "// script.mjs"
					}
					- "\nregisterFake"
					c- p="" {
						- "("
					}
					c- t="" {
						- "'negation-processor'"
					}
					c- p="" {
						- ","
